REV.page({"course":"english2","id":"all","title":"English 2: all weeks","summary":"The whole course on one page, for the last days before the exam: figures of speech and the sounds of speech; grammar in depth (adverbs, tense and aspect, passives, clauses, modals, questions, conditionals, quantifiers); and writing and speaking for study and work (arguments, essays, SOPs, précis, reports, e-mails and interviews).","n":0,"lectures":[],"toc":[{"id":"the-course-at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"The course at a glance"},{"id":"week-1-figures-of-speech-discourse-markers-rhythm-intonation","level":2,"title":"Week 1: Figures of speech, discourse markers, rhythm, intonation and language use"},{"id":"sixteen-figures-of-speech-1-1-1-2","level":3,"title":"Sixteen figures of speech (1.1, 1.2)"},{"id":"discourse-markers-1-3","level":3,"title":"Discourse markers (1.3)"},{"id":"rhythm-and-pitch-1-4","level":3,"title":"Rhythm and pitch (1.4)"},{"id":"intonation-1-5","level":3,"title":"Intonation (1.5)"},{"id":"language-use-1-6","level":3,"title":"Language use (1.6)"},{"id":"week-2-adverbs-tense-and-aspect-passives-adjectives","level":2,"title":"Week 2: Adverbs, tense and aspect, passives, adjectives"},{"id":"six-types-of-adverbs-2-1","level":3,"title":"Six types of adverbs (2.1)"},{"id":"tense-and-aspect-2-2","level":3,"title":"Tense and aspect (2.2)"},{"id":"passives-how-and-why-2-3","level":3,"title":"Passives: how and why (2.3)"},{"id":"adjectives-2-4","level":3,"title":"Adjectives (2.4)"},{"id":"degree-adverbs-2-5","level":3,"title":"Degree adverbs (2.5)"},{"id":"comment-viewpoint-and-focus-adverbs-2-6","level":3,"title":"Comment, viewpoint and focus adverbs (2.6)"},{"id":"week-3-clauses-complex-sentences-complements-and-adjuncts","level":2,"title":"Week 3: Clauses, complex sentences, complements and adjuncts"},{"id":"simple-compound-and-complex-sentences-3-1-3-3","level":3,"title":"Simple, compound and complex sentences (3.1, 3.3)"},{"id":"three-kinds-of-subordinate-clause-3-1","level":3,"title":"Three kinds of subordinate clause (3.1)"},{"id":"finite-and-non-finite-clauses-3-2","level":3,"title":"Finite and non-finite clauses (3.2)"},{"id":"the-adjectival-relative-clause-3-4","level":3,"title":"The adjectival (relative) clause (3.4)"},{"id":"complements-and-adjuncts-3-5","level":3,"title":"Complements and adjuncts (3.5)"},{"id":"week-4-writing-to-argue-instruct-and-influence-reflective-es","level":2,"title":"Week 4: Writing to argue, instruct and influence; reflective essays"},{"id":"writing-to-argue-4-1","level":3,"title":"Writing to argue (4.1)"},{"id":"the-structure-of-an-argument-4-2","level":3,"title":"The structure of an argument (4.2)"},{"id":"a-lab-manual-4-3","level":3,"title":"A lab manual (4.3)"},{"id":"writing-to-influence-4-4","level":3,"title":"Writing to influence (4.4)"},{"id":"reflective-essays-4-5-4-6","level":3,"title":"Reflective essays (4.5, 4.6)"},{"id":"week-5-mood-and-modal-verbs","level":2,"title":"Week 5: Mood and modal verbs"},{"id":"modal-auxiliaries-and-mood-5-1","level":3,"title":"Modal auxiliaries and mood (5.1)"},{"id":"which-modal-when-5-2-5-6","level":3,"title":"Which modal when (5.2–5.6)"},{"id":"week-6-softening-question-tags-questions-and-embedded-clause","level":2,"title":"Week 6: Softening, question tags, questions and embedded clauses"},{"id":"softening-6-1","level":3,"title":"Softening (6.1)"},{"id":"negatives-and-question-tags-6-2","level":3,"title":"Negatives and question tags (6.2)"},{"id":"questions-6-3","level":3,"title":"Questions (6.3)"},{"id":"embedded-questions-and-clauses-6-4-6-6","level":3,"title":"Embedded questions and clauses (6.4–6.6)"},{"id":"week-7-radio-reports-numbers-interviews-and-group-discussion","level":2,"title":"Week 7: Radio reports, numbers, interviews and group discussions"},{"id":"the-radio-report-7-1","level":3,"title":"The radio report (7.1)"},{"id":"presenting-numbers-7-2-7-3","level":3,"title":"Presenting numbers (7.2, 7.3)"},{"id":"at-an-interview-7-4","level":3,"title":"At an interview (7.4)"},{"id":"group-discussions-and-introductions-7-5-7-6","level":3,"title":"Group discussions and introductions (7.5, 7.6)"},{"id":"week-8-statement-of-purpose-pre-cis-and-lab-reports","level":2,"title":"Week 8: Statement of purpose, précis and lab reports"},{"id":"the-statement-of-purpose-8-1","level":3,"title":"The statement of purpose (8.1)"},{"id":"simple-words-fewer-words-8-2","level":3,"title":"Simple words, fewer words (8.2)"},{"id":"the-pre-cis-8-3-8-4","level":3,"title":"The précis (8.3, 8.4)"},{"id":"the-lab-report-8-5","level":3,"title":"The lab report (8.5)"},{"id":"week-9-punctuation-and-the-four-language-skills","level":2,"title":"Week 9: Punctuation and the four language skills"},{"id":"the-comma-9-1","level":3,"title":"The comma (9.1)"},{"id":"semicolon-colon-and-apostrophe-9-2","level":3,"title":"Semicolon, colon and apostrophe (9.2)"},{"id":"effective-communication-9-3","level":3,"title":"Effective communication (9.3)"},{"id":"speaking-9-4","level":3,"title":"Speaking (9.4)"},{"id":"listening-and-reading-9-5-9-6","level":3,"title":"Listening and reading (9.5, 9.6)"},{"id":"writing-coder-and-power-9-7-9-8","level":3,"title":"Writing: CODER and POWER (9.7, 9.8)"},{"id":"week-10-passives-participles-negatives-reported-speech-condi","level":2,"title":"Week 10: Passives, participles, negatives, reported speech, conditionals"},{"id":"passives-hiding-the-doer-10-1","level":3,"title":"Passives: hiding the doer (10.1)"},{"id":"participles-and-gerunds-10-2","level":3,"title":"Participles and gerunds (10.2)"},{"id":"negation-and-negative-sensitive-words-10-3","level":3,"title":"Negation and negative-sensitive words (10.3)"},{"id":"reported-speech-10-4","level":3,"title":"Reported speech (10.4)"},{"id":"conditionals-10-5","level":3,"title":"Conditionals (10.5)"},{"id":"week-11-quantifiers-contrast-words-vocabulary-and-connecting","level":2,"title":"Week 11: Quantifiers, contrast words, vocabulary and connecting ideas"},{"id":"quantifiers-11-1-11-2","level":3,"title":"Quantifiers (11.1, 11.2)"},{"id":"contrast-words-11-3","level":3,"title":"Contrast words (11.3)"},{"id":"words-and-morphemes-11-4","level":3,"title":"Words and morphemes (11.4)"},{"id":"word-formation-processes-11-5","level":3,"title":"Word-formation processes (11.5)"},{"id":"connecting-ideas-11-6","level":3,"title":"Connecting ideas (11.6)"},{"id":"vocabulary-word-parts-and-loanwords-11-7-11-8","level":3,"title":"Vocabulary: word parts and loanwords (11.7, 11.8)"},{"id":"week-12-pragmatic-competence-context-writing-at-work-and-int","level":2,"title":"Week 12: Pragmatic competence, context, writing at work and interviews"},{"id":"pragmatic-competence-12-1","level":3,"title":"Pragmatic competence (12.1)"},{"id":"essays-and-research-reports-12-2","level":3,"title":"Essays and research reports (12.2)"},{"id":"decent-e-mails-12-3","level":3,"title":"Decent e-mails (12.3)"},{"id":"barriers-and-the-speaking-model-12-4-12-5","level":3,"title":"Barriers and the SPEAKING model (12.4, 12.5)"},{"id":"re-sume-star-and-interviews-12-6-12-10","level":3,"title":"Résumé, STAR and interviews (12.6–12.10)"},{"id":"how-it-all-fits-together","level":2,"title":"How it all fits together"},{"id":"where-the-tense-goes","level":3,"title":"Where the tense goes"},{"id":"joining-two-ideas","level":3,"title":"Joining two ideas"},{"id":"saying-it-politely","level":3,"title":"Saying it politely"},{"id":"which-piece-of-writing","level":3,"title":"Which piece of writing"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"the-course-at-a-glance\">The course at a glance</h2>\n<p>English 2 weaves three strands through its twelve weeks.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Grammar: how the mind builds a sentence.</strong> Every sentence is a <strong>subject</strong> plus a <strong>predicate</strong>, joined by <strong>agreement</strong> and <strong>tense</strong>. Most grammar lectures follow the tense: it sits on <em>be</em> in a passive (Weeks 2 and 10), it leaves the verb in negatives, tags and questions (Weeks 6 and 10), and it is absent from modals and participles (Weeks 5 and 10). Clauses sit inside clauses (Weeks 3 and 6), joined by commas, connectors and contrast words (Weeks 9 and 11).</li>\n<li><strong>Speaking and listening.</strong> Figures of speech, discourse markers and the music of speech (Week 1); radio reports, numbers, interviews and group discussions (Week 7); communication and the four skills (Week 9); context, barriers and job interviews (Week 12).</li>\n<li><strong>Writing.</strong> Arguing, instructing, influencing and reflecting (Week 4); the statement of purpose, the précis and the lab report (Week 8); punctuation, CODER and POWER (Week 9); essays, research reports, e-mails and the résumé (Week 12).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>One idea runs under all of it: a sentence must be <strong>accurate</strong> (grammatical) and <strong>appropriate</strong> (right for the listener and the context). Accuracy brings fluency, and grammar together with the knowledge of context builds <strong>communicative confidence</strong>. The lecturers ask you to understand the patterns, not to memorise rules.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Week</th>\n<th>Topics</th>\n<th>Key result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english2/w01\">1</a></td>\n<td>16 figures of speech, discourse markers, rhythm, pitch, intonation, language use</td>\n<td>English is stress-timed; a final rise turns a statement into a question</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english2/w02\">2</a></td>\n<td>adverbs, tense and aspect, passives, adjectives, degree, comment, viewpoint and focus adverbs</td>\n<td>3 tenses × 4 aspects = 12 forms; passive = <em>be</em> + V3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english2/w03\">3</a></td>\n<td>clauses; simple, compound, complex sentences; complements and adjuncts</td>\n<td>one verb with tense = one clause</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english2/w04\">4</a></td>\n<td>writing to argue, a lab manual, writing to influence, reflective essays</td>\n<td>situation, one side, the other side, conclusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english2/w05\">5</a></td>\n<td>modal auxiliaries and mood: can, could, will, would, must, shall, should, may, might</td>\n<td>modals carry no tense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english2/w06\">6</a></td>\n<td>softening, question tags, questions, embedded questions, <em>whether/if</em>, <em>that</em>-clauses</td>\n<td>questions front the tense; embedded questions do not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english2/w07\">7</a></td>\n<td>radio reports, presenting numbers, interviews, group discussions, introductions</td>\n<td><strong>THIR</strong>-ty but thir-<strong>TEEN</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english2/w08\">8</a></td>\n<td>statement of purpose, précis, lab report</td>\n<td>a précis keeps every point in one-third of the words</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english2/w09\">9</a></td>\n<td>comma, semicolon, colon, apostrophe; communication; the four skills; CODER and POWER</td>\n<td>dependent + independent: comma; independent + dependent: none</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english2/w10\">10</a></td>\n<td>passives, participles, negative-sensitive words, reported speech, conditionals</td>\n<td>negation = tense + <em>not</em> + verb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english2/w11\">11</a></td>\n<td>quantifiers, contrast words, morphemes, word formation, connectors, loanwords</td>\n<td><em>a few</em> = some; <em>few</em> = hardly any</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/english2/w12\">12</a></td>\n<td>pragmatic competence, essays and reports, e-mails, barriers, SPEAKING, résumé, interviews</td>\n<td>STAR: Situation, Task, Action, Result</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Each week below gives the ideas, the rules (in definition boxes), the lectures’ own example sentences, a worked example of each main technique, and the common mistakes. After each topic, <strong>On the week page</strong> links take you to the full sections. The last part ties the weeks together in “which form when” tables and ends with mixed questions.</p>\n<p>A few signs used on this page: ✗ marks a wrong sentence and ✓ a right one; → means “becomes”; <strong>V3</strong> is the past participle, the third form of a verb (<em>written, played</em>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-1-figures-of-speech-discourse-markers-rhythm-intonation\">Week 1: Figures of speech, discourse markers, rhythm, intonation and language use</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Week 1 is about meaning that goes beyond the plain words. <strong>Figures of speech</strong> (1.1, 1.2) say an idea indirectly, so that it has more impact. <strong>Discourse markers</strong> (1.3) are the small words that open, link, turn and close our talk. <strong>Rhythm, pitch and intonation</strong> (1.4, 1.5) carry meaning through the voice. <strong>Language use</strong> (1.6) looks at English in multilingual India.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"sixteen-figures-of-speech-1-1-1-2\">Sixteen figures of speech (1.1, 1.2)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Figure of speech</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>figure of speech</strong> is a literary device that presents an idea <strong>without directly referring to it</strong>. It is not meant <strong>literally</strong> (word for word): in <em>He is as modest as a hermit</em>, he is not a hermit. Figures of speech compare or link otherwise unrelated things, occur in speech and in writing, and give a <strong>rhetorical</strong> effect (they impress or persuade). There is no complete list and no fixed order: we learn them by noticing them in use (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SrYTLUUfrpQ&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The sixteen figures of the two lectures, with the lectures’ own examples:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Figure: what it does</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Simile</strong>: compares two unrelated things with <em>like</em> or <em>as</em></td>\n<td><em>O my Luve is like a red, red rose</em>; <em>as modest as a hermit</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Metaphor</strong>: says A <strong>is</strong> B, with no <em>like</em> or <em>as</em></td>\n<td><em>Life is a roller-coaster ride.</em> <em>Silence is golden.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Personification</strong>: gives a thing or an idea human qualities</td>\n<td><em>The stars winked at us from the distant black sky.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Paradox</strong>: sounds impossible but is true (opposite <strong>ideas</strong>)</td>\n<td><em>I must be cruel to be kind.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Oxymoron</strong>: two opposite <strong>words</strong> side by side</td>\n<td><em>a bittersweet experience</em>; <em>sweet sorrow</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Antithesis</strong>: opposites in the <strong>same grammar pattern</strong></td>\n<td><em>No pain, no gain.</em> <em>Man proposes, God disposes.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alliteration</strong>: the same first sound in neighbouring words</td>\n<td><em>Betty baked buns besides bacons</em>; <em>Coca Cola</em>, <em>PayPal</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Irony</strong>: the outcome is the opposite of what we expect</td>\n<td>a nurse who is scared of blood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pun</strong>: one word with two meanings at once</td>\n<td><em>Can February March? No, but April May.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Juxtaposition</strong>: opposite ideas placed side by side</td>\n<td><em>It was the best of times, it was the worst of times.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Synecdoche</strong>: a part for the whole, or the whole for a part</td>\n<td><em>He fell fast asleep on the wheels.</em> <em>a headcount</em>; <em>the police</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Anaphora</strong>: the same word at the start of clauses or lines</td>\n<td><em>Stay home, Stay safe.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Metonymy</strong>: the name of a closely related thing</td>\n<td><em>Delhi</em> (the Government of India); <em>the White House</em>; <em>the Kiwis</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Litotes</strong>: a negative statement with a positive meaning</td>\n<td><em>Not bad.</em> <em>What he said is not without truth.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hyperbole</strong>: exaggeration, for emphasis</td>\n<td><em>It’s raining cats and dogs.</em> <em>I have been waiting forever.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Euphemism</strong>: a mild word for something unpleasant or taboo</td>\n<td><em>passed away</em> (died); <em>downsized</em> (fired)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Assonance</strong>, <strong>consonance</strong> and <strong>sibilance</strong> are forms of alliteration whose repeated sounds need not be at the start of the words, or next to each other (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). (Slashes, as in /s/, mean “the sound”, not the letter.)</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>What repeats</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>assonance</td>\n<td>a <strong>vowel</strong> sound, anywhere</td>\n<td><em>Jack and Jill went up the hill</em> (the <em>i</em> sound)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>consonance</td>\n<td>a <strong>consonant</strong> sound; at the ends of words it makes a <strong>rhyme</strong></td>\n<td><em>Too cool for school</em> (<em>l</em>); <em>bells … tells</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sibilance</td>\n<td>/s/ or /sh/</td>\n<td><em>She sells sea shells on the seashore.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Antithesis and oxymoron both fall under juxtaposition. The lecture’s last slide adds a caution: use figures of speech “creatively”, but stay sensitive to socio-cultural groups, “to avoid bias in any form” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Ask these questions in order</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Is something <strong>compared</strong> with an unrelated thing? With <em>like</em> or <em>as</em>: simile. Without them: metaphor. Given human actions or feelings: personification.</li>\n<li>Is one <strong>name</strong> used for another? A part or the whole: synecdoche. A related thing: metonymy.</li>\n<li>Are <strong>opposites</strong> together? Two words: oxymoron. An idea that sounds impossible but is true: paradox. A matching grammar pattern: antithesis. Simply side by side: juxtaposition.</li>\n<li>Is a <strong>sound</strong> repeated? The first sound: alliteration. A vowel: assonance. A consonant: consonance. /s/ or /sh/: sibilance.</li>\n<li>Is a whole <strong>word</strong> repeated at the start? Anaphora.</li>\n<li>One word, <strong>two meanings</strong>? Pun.</li>\n<li>The <strong>opposite outcome</strong>? Irony.</li>\n<li>Is the truth <strong>resized</strong>? Bigger: hyperbole. Smaller, with <em>not</em>: litotes. Softer: euphemism.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Hooks for the names</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Simile</em> sounds like <em>similar</em>. <em>Hyper-</em> means over: over the top. <em>Litotes</em> sounds like <em>little</em>: say a little to mean more. <em>Oxymoron</em> is Greek for “sharp-dull”, itself an oxymoron. Latin <em>juxta</em> means next to. <em>Euphemism</em> is “good speech”. Sibilance <strong>hisses</strong>. Meto-<strong>nym</strong>-y borrows a <strong>name</strong>. Synecdoche is a <strong>s</strong>lice for the whole.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w01/what-is-a-figure-of-speech\">what a figure of speech is</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/simile\">simile</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/metaphor\">metaphor</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/personification\">personification</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/paradox\">paradox</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/oxymoron\">oxymoron</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/antithesis\">antithesis</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/alliteration\">alliteration</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/assonance-consonance-and-sibilance\">assonance, consonance and sibilance</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/irony\">irony</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/pun\">pun</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/juxtaposition\">juxtaposition</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/synecdoche\">synecdoche</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/anaphora\">anaphora</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/metonymy\">metonymy</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/litotes\">litotes</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/hyperbole\">hyperbole</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/euphemism\">euphemism</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/which-figure-is-it-a-quick-route\">a quick route</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"discourse-markers-1-3\">Discourse markers (1.3)</h3>\n<p>Language is built in four levels: sounds, then words, then sentences, then <strong>discourse</strong>: sentences used in a context. A discourse needs at least two sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gEzyD_f706w&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Discourse markers</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Discourse markers</strong> (<em>so, well, you know, actually, anyway</em> …) are <strong>connectors</strong> that join ideas, sentences or phrases. They are sometimes called <strong>fillers</strong>, but they make a speaker <strong>fluent</strong> and <strong>coherent</strong> (clear and well connected). In speech they avoid repetition, give a thinking pause or fill a gap. They mostly come <strong>at the start</strong>, but they can also shift or end a conversation, and they show the speaker’s mood or attitude. They can be conjunctions, adverbs or <strong>interjections</strong> (short exclamations such as <em>Oh!</em>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gEzyD_f706w&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Meera and Kshitij (the lecture’s conversation at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gEzyD_f706w&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>“<strong>So</strong>, I want to tell you something.” “<strong>Oh!</strong> What is it?” <em>So</em> opens gently; <em>Oh!</em> sounds interested.</li>\n<li>“<strong>You know</strong>, I bought a villa in ECR last week. <strong>I mean, like</strong>, there is an EMI.” <em>You know, I mean, like</em> connect two parts of the talk. (An EMI is a monthly loan instalment.)</li>\n<li>“<strong>Right.</strong>” stands alone, and “<strong>Anyway</strong>, you have a house of your own now” turns the talk to a new point.</li>\n<li>Without the markers (“I want to tell you something.” “What is it?”), the talk sounds abrupt and artificial.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> markers begin a conversation, stand alone, connect (<em>you know, I mean, um</em>) and change the focus (<em>well, OK, anyway</em>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gEzyD_f706w&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Markers (the lecture’s lists)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>start a conversation</td>\n<td><em>well, first of all, to begin with, for starters, at the outset</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a positive opinion</td>\n<td><em>wow, absolutely, yeah, yep</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a negative opinion</td>\n<td><em>honestly, to be honest</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an unsure opinion</td>\n<td><em>I guess so, I suppose so</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>add information</td>\n<td><em>to my knowledge, as far as I know, on top of that, another thing</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>emphasise</td>\n<td><em>actually, in fact, as a matter of fact, apparently, hands down</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>give an example</td>\n<td><em>for instance, for example, you can see this with</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>clarify</td>\n<td><em>you see this?, you understand this?, did you get that?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>contrast</td>\n<td><em>that said, at the same time</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>conclude</td>\n<td><em>overall, all in all, in a nutshell, at the end of the day</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A marker tells the listener in advance what kind of sentence is coming: <em>Honestly, I did not like the movie.</em> · <em>This is hands down the best pizza I have had in my life.</em> · <em>In a nutshell, the taxes may increase at least by five per cent.</em></p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w01/what-discourse-is\">what discourse is</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/what-discourse-markers-are\">what markers are</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/a-conversation-with-and-without-markers\">the conversation</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/markers-by-use\">markers by use</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rhythm-and-pitch-1-4\">Rhythm and pitch (1.4)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Syllable, stress and rhythm</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>syllable</strong> is a unit of a word with a <strong>vowel</strong> at its centre: <em>tell</em> (one), <em>mourn-ful</em> (two), <em>con-duc-tor</em> (three).</li>\n<li><strong>Stress</strong> is the <strong>prominence</strong> of a syllable: it is said with more force, so it stands out.</li>\n<li><strong>Rhythm</strong> is the repeated pattern of sounds: the arrangement of syllables in a unit of speech. The groups of words between two pauses take about the same time to say. Without rhythm, speech sounds <strong>monotonous</strong> (on one flat note), like a robot’s (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>David Crystal, quoted in the lecture (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Language</th>\n<th>Its rhythm comes from</th>\n<th>Sounds like</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>English</td>\n<td>stressed syllables at roughly regular intervals: <strong>stress-timed</strong></td>\n<td>tum-te-tum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>French</td>\n<td>every syllable in a steady flow: <strong>syllable-timed</strong></td>\n<td>rat-a-tat-a-tat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Latin</td>\n<td>long and short syllables</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>many oriental languages</td>\n<td>pitch height (high or low)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Tapping out an English line (the line in Crystal’s quote)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>The curfew tolls the knell of parting day.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Split it into syllables: the-cur-few-tolls-the-knell-of-part-ing-day. That is 10.</li>\n<li>The stressed syllables (bold on the slide): <strong>cur</strong>, <strong>tolls</strong>, <strong>knell</strong>, <strong>part</strong>, <strong>day</strong>. That is 5.</li>\n<li>Weak and strong alternate: te-<strong>TUM</strong> te-<strong>TUM</strong> te-<strong>TUM</strong> te-<strong>TUM</strong> te-<strong>TUM</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 5 beats at roughly regular intervals, with weak syllables between them: a stress-timed rhythm.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Stress also changes meaning. In <em>I thought your brother was a bus conductor</em>, stress on <strong>brother</strong> means “not your sister”, on <strong>was</strong> “I didn’t know he still is a bus conductor”, on <strong>bus</strong> “I thought he was an orchestral conductor”, and on <strong>conductor</strong> “not a bus driver” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>). In Longfellow’s <em>A Psalm of Life</em> the lines have 8, 7, 8 and 7 syllables, so each takes about the same time to say.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Pitch</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Pitch</strong> is how high or low the voice is. It depends on the <strong>frequency</strong> with which the vocal cords vibrate (how many times a second they shake) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>). A low pitch can show sadness, shock, dullness or guilt; a high pitch, excitement, joy, triumph, and even anger.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Using pitch</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Keep your pitch balanced and varied, so that you are not monotonous. Say <em>thank you</em> flatly, then warmly: the words are the same, the meaning is not. Avoid raising the pitch at the end of a statement, because a final rise can turn it into a question (Lecture 1.5). The aim is <strong>communicative confidence</strong>, and “we reach fluency through accuracy” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w01/rhythm-means-flow\">rhythm means flow</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/english-is-stress-timed\">English is stress-timed</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/stress-changes-meaning\">stress changes meaning</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/rhythm-in-poems-and-songs\">poems and songs</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/pitch\">pitch</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"intonation-1-5\">Intonation (1.5)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Intonation</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Intonation</strong> is the <strong>melody</strong> of speech, made by changing the pitch of the voice. It is a feature of pronunciation, so it shows only in speech, and it is about <strong>how</strong> we say something (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uE_v3pZFFe0&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). Intonation can be <strong>rising</strong>, <strong>falling</strong> or <strong>flat</strong>. A flat tune is used for statements; a <strong>rise at the end</strong> makes a sentence a question. (Two related terms: <strong>tone</strong> is the manner of sounds, high or low; <strong>pitch</strong> is the relative height of tones.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“John is a doctor”: statement or question? (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uE_v3pZFFe0&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Said with a flat tune, <em>John is a doctor.</em> is a statement of fact.</li>\n<li>Now let the voice rise on the last word: <em>John is a <strong>doctor</strong>?</em></li>\n<li>The words and their order have not changed; only the final tune has.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the final rise turns the statement into a question. In the same way, in <em>Did you want to go home?</em> a rise on <em>home</em>, <em>go</em>, <em>want</em> or <em>you</em> puts that word in focus and resolves the <strong>ambiguity</strong> (more than one possible meaning) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uE_v3pZFFe0&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>What intonation does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>grammatical</td>\n<td>marks clauses, sentences, statements and questions; resolves ambiguity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>attitudinal</td>\n<td>shows shock, surprise, anger, sarcasm, pleasure, interest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>discourse</td>\n<td>marks the start and the conclusion of a talk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>focus</td>\n<td>puts one part of the message in focus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>psychological</td>\n<td>makes things easy to remember</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w01/intonation\">intonation</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/five-related-terms\">five related terms</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/rising-falling-and-flat\">rising, falling and flat</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/what-intonation-does\">what intonation does</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"language-use-1-6\">Language use (1.6)</h3>\n<p>A <strong>language family</strong> is a group of languages that come from a common ancestor. India has five (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Family</th>\n<th>Lecture examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Indo-Aryan</td>\n<td>Hindi, Assamese (the north)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dravidian</td>\n<td>Tamil, Malayalam, Telugu (the south)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Austro-Asiatic (Munda)</td>\n<td>Santali, Mundari, Ho, Sora</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tibeto-Burman</td>\n<td>Tibetic, Tani, Naga</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Andamanese</td>\n<td>Jarawa, Sentinelese, Aka-Kol</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Languages borrow and mix.</strong> A Malayalam-speaking child’s sentences contain English words (<em>one, two, sticker</em>) and <strong>loan words</strong>, borrowed words such as Arabic <em>baaki</em> and Hebrew <em>ba</em>. <strong>Reduplication</strong>, repeating a word for emphasis (Hindi <em>dheere dheere</em>), passes into Indian English: <em>She is walking slowly, slowly.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Three myths</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>). “An early start is the ideal condition”: the quality of the input matters more, and adults are slower mainly because of <strong>inhibitions</strong> (shame, fear of errors). “The mother tongue stands in the way”: false; it <strong>aids</strong> learning. “A child should hear only English”: false; multilingualism helps thinking.</li>\n<li><strong>English in India.</strong> English is “nobody’s language”, so it works as a neutral <strong>link language</strong>. Hindi is the <strong>official</strong> language and English the <strong>associate official</strong> language; Hindi is not the national language. English stayed alive through fluidity and openness (Hinglish, Manglish, Tanglish), unlike the elite languages Sanskrit, Latin and Greek.</li>\n<li><strong>Indian English</strong> has its own features, from the influence of the first language: reduplication; a retroflex <em>t</em>; articles (<em>We are going to temple</em>); <em>I know what is your name</em> (for <em>what your name is</em>); tags like <em>It is beautiful, no?</em> “Our English is not wrong. We speak differently.” The one mantra: <strong>the more the input, the better the output</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w01/india-s-language-families\">language families</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/languages-borrow-and-mix\">languages borrow and mix</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/language-in-school-three-myths\">three myths</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/english-in-india\">English in India</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w01/indian-english\">Indian English</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 1</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Not every <em>like</em> or <em>as</em> makes a simile: <em>I like tea</em> and <em>as soon as possible</em> compare nothing.</li>\n<li>Oxymoron or paradox? Two opposite <strong>words</strong> (<em>sweet sorrow</em>) make an oxymoron; an opposite <strong>idea</strong> that is true (<em>I must be cruel to be kind</em>) makes a paradox.</li>\n<li>Metonymy or synecdoche? Ask: is it a <strong>piece</strong> of the thing? <em>Wheels</em> for a car is synecdoche; <em>the White House</em> for the President is metonymy.</li>\n<li>Euphemism or litotes? A euphemism softens an unpleasant word (<em>passed away</em>); litotes uses a negative to say something positive (<em>not bad</em>).</li>\n<li>Irony is an unexpected outcome and needs no speaker. Sarcasm, spoken mockery, is not one of the sixteen.</li>\n<li>In the alliteration family the <strong>sound</strong> counts, not the spelling: <em>Coca Cola</em> and <em>kitkat</em> repeat the same /k/.</li>\n<li>A “filler” is not useless: markers make talk fluent and coherent.</li>\n<li>A rise at the end of a statement can turn it into a question.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w01\">Week 1</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-2-adverbs-tense-and-aspect-passives-adjectives\">Week 2: Adverbs, tense and aspect, passives, adjectives</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Week 2 looks closely at the verb and the words around it. <strong>Tense</strong> says <em>when</em>; <strong>aspect</strong> says what <em>state</em> the action is in; the <strong>passive</strong> moves the doer out of the way. Adjectives and adverbs add detail, degree, comment and focus. The lecturers’ promise: see how these markers work, and you gain <strong>accuracy</strong>; accuracy builds <strong>confidence</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A few terms first. The <strong>subject</strong> is who or what a sentence is about (<em><strong>Maya</strong> runs</em>); the <strong>predicate</strong> is the rest, built around the verb; the <strong>object</strong> receives the action (<em>Deepa wrote <strong>a novel</strong></em>). A <strong>clause</strong> has its own subject and verb; a <strong>phrase</strong> does not.</p>\n<h3 id=\"six-types-of-adverbs-2-1\">Six types of adverbs (2.1)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adverb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>adverb</strong> modifies (adds information to) a verb, an adjective or another adverb: it tells when, where, how, or how much. An <strong>adverb clause</strong> has its own subject and verb (<em>He carried the load <strong>until he was tired</strong></em>); an <strong>adverb phrase</strong> does not (<em>You are irritated <strong>without any reason</strong></em>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QGkHH_dXtfM&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Question it answers</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>manner</td>\n<td>How?</td>\n<td><em>Hold it <strong>carefully</strong>.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>degree</td>\n<td>How much?</td>\n<td><em>Jessy <strong>completely</strong> forgot about her appointment.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>place, direction</td>\n<td>Where?</td>\n<td><em>I went <strong>through the jungle</strong>.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>time, frequency</td>\n<td>When? How often?</td>\n<td><em>He <strong>always</strong> gets a good result.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sentence adverb</td>\n<td>the speaker’s view of the whole sentence; at the front, with a comma</td>\n<td><em><strong>Hopefully</strong>, I will finish the assignment.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>conjunctive adverb</td>\n<td>links two clauses and shows how they relate (a <strong>connector</strong>)</td>\n<td><em>Last year there was little rain; <strong>consequently</strong>, we did not have a good harvest.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Ask the question, not the ending</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Completely</em> and <em>thoroughly</em> end in <em>-ly</em> like manner adverbs, but they answer “how much?”, so they are adverbs of <strong>degree</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QGkHH_dXtfM&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w02/adverb-clauses-and-adverb-phrases\">adverb clauses and phrases</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/six-types-of-adverbs\">six types of adverbs</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"tense-and-aspect-2-2\">Tense and aspect (2.2)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Tense and aspect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tense</strong> is the <strong>time</strong> of an action: present, past or future. It is carried by the first helping verb (an <strong>auxiliary</strong>: <em>is, was, will, has, had</em> …) or by the verb itself (<em>see, saw</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Aspect</strong> is the <strong>state</strong> of the event: <strong>indefinite</strong> (a habit or a settled fact), <strong>continuous</strong> (in progress: <em>be</em> + <em>-ing</em>), <strong>perfect</strong> (complete: <em>have</em> + V3) and <strong>perfect continuous</strong> (complete after going on: <em>have been</em> + <em>-ing</em>).</li>\n<li>3 tenses × 4 aspects = <strong>12 forms</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Present</th>\n<th>Past</th>\n<th>Future</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>indefinite</td>\n<td>play / plays</td>\n<td>played</td>\n<td>will play</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>continuous</td>\n<td>am / is / are playing</td>\n<td>was / were playing</td>\n<td>will be playing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>perfect</td>\n<td>has / have played</td>\n<td>had played</td>\n<td>will have played</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>perfect continuous</td>\n<td>has / have been playing</td>\n<td>had been playing</td>\n<td>will have been playing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecture’s sentences: <em>The girls <strong>are playing</strong> in the ground.</em> · <em>The girls <strong>had played</strong> effectively.</em> · <em>I <strong>will have typed</strong> a mail.</em> · <em>The girls <strong>will have been playing</strong> nicely.</em></p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Reading a verb group from left to right (the lecture’s reading at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>The girls had been playing effectively.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The first helper carries the tense: <em>had</em> is <strong>past</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>had been</em> is <em>have</em> + <em>been</em>: <strong>perfect</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>playing</em> ends in <em>-ing</em>: <strong>continuous</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> past perfect continuous. In the same way, <em>will have been playing</em> = <em>will</em> (future) + <em>have been</em> (perfect) + <em>-ing</em> (continuous): future perfect continuous.</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>In <em>he lives</em>, the <em>-s</em> is an <strong>agreement</strong> marker (it comes only with <em>he, she, it</em>), not a tense marker: <em>I live, they live</em> have no marker at all.</li>\n<li><em>I am tired</em> has tense but no aspect: there is “no aspect without a verb” (an event).</li>\n<li><strong>Regular</strong> verbs add <em>-d</em> or <em>-ed</em> (<em>act, acted, acted</em>). <strong>Irregular</strong> verbs change inside (<em>begin, began, begun</em>), do not change (<em>cut, cut, cut</em>), or become a new word (<em>go, went, gone</em>: <strong>suppletion</strong>). The <em>-ed</em> form is both the past tense (<em>I acted</em>) and the past participle (<em>I have acted</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w02/three-tenses\">three tenses</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/four-aspects\">four aspects</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/all-twelve-forms\">all twelve forms</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/regular-and-irregular-verb-forms\">regular and irregular verbs</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"passives-how-and-why-2-3\">Passives: how and why (2.3)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The passive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In a <strong>passive</strong> sentence the subject <strong>receives</strong> the action. The standard pattern (the <strong>canonical</strong> passive) is a form of <strong>be</strong> (or <strong>get</strong>) + <strong>V3</strong> of a <strong>transitive</strong> verb, a verb that takes an object. The doer, the <strong>agent</strong>, can be added with <em>by</em>, or left out (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Deepa wrote a novel</em> → <em>A novel was written by Deepa</em> (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>In the active, <em>Deepa</em> is the subject (the agent), <em>wrote</em> the verb (past) and <em>a novel</em> the object.</li>\n<li>The verb becomes <em>be</em> in the same tense + V3: <em>wrote</em> → <em>was written</em>. <em>Written</em> is now only a participle: it has no tense and takes no object.</li>\n<li>English needs a subject (<em>It is raining</em>, <em>There was a king</em>; only an order like <em>Get out!</em> drops it), so <em>a novel</em> moves into the empty subject slot.</li>\n<li>Add the agent with <em>by</em> if you want: <em>by Deepa</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>A novel was written (by Deepa).</em> <em>A novel</em> is now the <strong>grammatical</strong> subject, but Deepa is still the <strong>agent</strong>: in a passive, the grammatical roles and the roles in the event split.</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>Only transitive verbs have passives: <em>Ravi runs very fast</em> has none (<em>very fast</em> is an adverb, not an object).</li>\n<li>With two objects, the word order decides: <em>Ravi gave <strong>Deepa</strong> a book</em> → <em><strong>Deepa</strong> was given a book</em>; <em>Ravi gave <strong>a book</strong> to Deepa</em> → <em><strong>A book</strong> was given to Deepa</em>.</li>\n<li>Any tense works: <em>She <strong>has written</strong> a note</em> → <em>A note <strong>has been written</strong> (by her)</em>; <em>Engineers <strong>are repairing</strong> the bridge</em> → <em>The bridge <strong>is being repaired</strong></em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Why use a passive (the lecture’s list, from <em>Garner’s Modern English Usage</em>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Reason</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>the receiver matters most</td>\n<td><em>America was discovered by Columbus.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the agent is unknown</td>\n<td><em>My watch was stolen.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the agent is not important</td>\n<td><em>The bridge is being repaired.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the agent is obvious</td>\n<td><em>The workers are paid weekly.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>announcements and appeals</td>\n<td><em>People are requested to donate generously to the relief funds.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>to avoid responsibility</td>\n<td><em>The bullet got fired.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>extra stress on the agent, at the end</td>\n<td><em>The patient was killed by the doctor.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w02/passives-how-they-are-built\">how passives are built</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/grammatical-roles-and-thematic-roles\">grammatical and thematic roles</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/only-transitive-verbs-have-a-passive\">transitive verbs only</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/passives-with-two-objects\">two objects</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/passives-in-any-tense\">any tense</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/why-we-use-the-passive\">why we use the passive</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"adjectives-2-4\">Adjectives (2.4)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>An adjective can tell size, shape, age, colour, origin, material, opinion, observation or purpose: <em>a <strong>big</strong> man, a <strong>round</strong> cup, an <strong>iron</strong> bridge, a <strong>reading</strong> hall</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Typical endings help you spot adjectives: <em>-able/-ible, -al, -ful, -ic, -ive, -less, -ous, -some</em> (<em>responsible, harmful, careless, dangerous, awesome</em>). They are clues, not rules: <em>patient</em> is a noun in <em>The patient was killed by the doctor</em>.</li>\n<li>A noun can work as an adjective, a <strong>noun modifier</strong>: <em>dinner table, music class</em>. An adjective comes before it: <em>an <strong>expensive</strong> music system</em>.</li>\n<li>Positions that show an adjective: just before a noun (<em>red dress</em>), or between an article, a possessive, a demonstrative or an amount and the noun (<em>the <strong>dirty</strong> room, Ram’s <strong>white</strong> shoes, that <strong>aggressive</strong> moment, a few <strong>ordinary</strong> things</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Attributive and predicative adjectives</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>attributive</strong> adjective goes before the noun: <em>I have an <strong>old</strong> car.</em></li>\n<li>A <strong>predicative</strong> adjective comes after a linking verb (<em>be, look, seem, become</em>) and completes the sense, as a <strong>complement</strong>: <em>This place seems <strong>unsafe</strong>.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some adjectives change meaning with the position: <em>her <strong>late</strong> husband</em> (dead) / <em>always <strong>late</strong></em> (not on time); <em><strong>certain</strong> reasons</em> (some) / <em>I was <strong>certain</strong></em> (sure); <em>this <strong>particular</strong> work</em> (this one) / <em>My father is very <strong>particular</strong></em> (hard to please) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Attached or in the predicate</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>A</strong>ttributive adjectives are <strong>a</strong>ttached to the noun (<em>a big book</em>). <strong>P</strong>redicative adjectives sit in the <strong>p</strong>redicate (<em>This book is big</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The order with a degree adverb is article, degree adverb, adjective, noun: <em>a <strong>very</strong> cold day</em> (✗ <em>very a cold day</em>). <em>Quite</em> usually comes before the article (<em><strong>quite an</strong> expensive item</em>), and <em>rather</em> after it (<em>a <strong>rather</strong> painful decision</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w02/what-adjectives-tell-us\">what adjectives tell us</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/spotting-adjectives-by-their-endings\">endings</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/nouns-that-act-as-adjectives\">nouns as adjectives</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/attributive-and-predicative-adjectives\">attributive and predicative</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/same-adjective-different-meaning\">same adjective, different meaning</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/where-adjectives-sit-in-a-phrase\">positions</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/articles-and-degree-adverbs-before-an-adjective\">articles and degree adverbs</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"degree-adverbs-2-5\">Degree adverbs (2.5)</h3>\n<p>A <strong>degree adverb</strong> sets the extent of an adjective, a verb or another adverb: <em>They are <strong>extremely</strong> sad.</em> · <em>She is <strong>almost</strong> always late.</em></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Choice</th>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>very</em> or <em>too</em></td>\n<td><em>very</em> = a high degree; <em>too</em> = more than needed</td>\n<td><em>very</em> hot in Chennai, perfect for swimming / <em>too</em> hot to stay in this room</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>very</em> or <em>very much</em></td>\n<td><em>very</em> + adjective (also <em>-ing</em>/<em>-ed</em> adjectives); <em>very much</em> + feeling verbs and passive participles</td>\n<td><em>very</em> disappointing; <em>I very much agree</em>; <em>The new highway was very much needed.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>extremely</em> or <em>absolutely</em></td>\n<td><em>extremely, very, hugely</em> + gradable; <em>absolutely, completely, simply</em> + non-gradable</td>\n<td><em>extremely</em> difficult, <em>hugely</em> successful; <em>absolutely</em> sure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>quite</em></td>\n<td>“fairly” with gradable adjectives; “completely” with non-gradable ones</td>\n<td><em>quite</em> satisfied / <em>quite</em> wrong</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Gradable and non-gradable adjectives</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>gradable</strong> adjective has levels (<em>cold</em>: a bit cold, very cold, extremely cold). A <strong>non-gradable</strong> one has none (<em>finished</em>). Test: if “a bit ___” makes sense, the adjective is gradable (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uauQ5eO0Asg&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). Words that naturally go together, such as <em>absolutely sure</em>, are <strong>collocations</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w02/very-and-too\">very and too</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/very-and-very-much\">very and very much</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/gradable-and-non-gradable-adjectives\">gradable and non-gradable</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/quite-two-meanings\">quite</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"comment-viewpoint-and-focus-adverbs-2-6\">Comment, viewpoint and focus adverbs (2.6)</h3>\n<p>The verb is the “powerhouse” of the sentence: every added phrase in <em>John reads a book in the night with his friends after dinner</em> tells us about <em>reading</em>. Language skill has three parts: <strong>linguistic</strong> competence (the structure), <strong>communicative</strong> competence (using it with others) and <strong>pragmatic</strong> competence (understanding the context) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jyv6b3LU_pE&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>). These three kinds of adverbs come from the pragmatic side.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it adds</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>comment</td>\n<td>the speaker’s comment: likelihood, attitude or judgement</td>\n<td><em><strong>Frankly</strong>, I’m not surprised her family disowned her.</em> · <em>The plane <strong>apparently</strong> overshot the runway after landing.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>viewpoint</td>\n<td>the angle the speaker looks from</td>\n<td><em><strong>Financially</strong>, the hospitalisation has been a disaster for my neighbour.</em> · <em><strong>Technically</strong>, the two countries are still at war.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>focus</td>\n<td>puts one part in focus</td>\n<td><em>My brother has <strong>only</strong> brought some books.</em> / <em><strong>Only</strong> my brother has brought some food.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A focus adverb works on the phrase in its <strong>scope</strong>, usually the phrase right after it: <em><strong>Even</strong> Liya can speak English</em> (you might not expect her to) / <em>Liya can <strong>even</strong> speak German</em> (on top of everything else); <em>with luck <strong>alone</strong></em> means “only with luck”.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w02/comment-adverbs\">comment adverbs</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/viewpoint-adverbs\">viewpoint adverbs</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w02/focus-adverbs-only-even-alone\">focus adverbs</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 2</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>An <em>-ly</em> ending does not make a manner adverb: <em>completely</em> is an adverb of degree.</li>\n<li>Tense is not aspect, and perfect is not past: <em>I have typed a mail</em> is <strong>present</strong> tense with perfect aspect.</li>\n<li>The <em>-s</em> of <em>he lives</em> marks agreement, not tense.</li>\n<li>In a passive, keep the tense (<em>wrote</em> → <em>was written</em>, not <em>is written</em>), use V3 (✗ <em>was wrote</em>), and make <em>be</em> agree with the new subject (<em>Novels <strong>were</strong> written</em>).</li>\n<li>Mixing the two-object patterns: ✗ <em>A book was given Deepa.</em> ✗ <em>Deepa was given a book to.</em></li>\n<li>A passive of an intransitive verb (✗ for <em>Ravi runs very fast</em>).</li>\n<li>✗ <em>very a cold day</em> → <em>a very cold day</em>; ✗ <em>I very agree</em> → <em>I very much agree</em>; ✗ <em>She was very much disturbed</em> → <em>very disturbed</em>.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>absolutely successful</em> → <em>hugely successful</em> (<em>successful</em> is gradable).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w02\">Week 2</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-3-clauses-complex-sentences-complements-and-adjuncts\">Week 3: Clauses, complex sentences, complements and adjuncts</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Every English sentence has two parts, a <strong>subject</strong> and a <strong>predicate</strong>. Long sentences are made by putting <strong>clauses</strong> inside clauses. Once you can find the subject, the verb and what fills each slot, you can see through even a very long sentence “like an X-ray machine”: the mind treats every complex sentence like a simple one. Week 3 names the kinds of clauses, and the parts a sentence must have.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Sentence, clause and phrase</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sentence</strong> = subject + predicate; <strong>predicate</strong> = verb + object(s) + adjuncts (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n<li>A <strong>clause</strong> has its own subject and a verb with <strong>tense</strong>: <em>which <strong>was</strong> only a corner</em>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>phrase</strong> has no subject and no verb with tense: <em>on the roof</em>, <em>a glass of water</em>.</li>\n<li>An <strong>independent</strong> clause (also <strong>main</strong> or <strong>matrix</strong> clause) can stand alone. A <strong>subordinate</strong> clause (also <strong>embedded</strong> or <strong>dependent</strong> clause) depends on a word of the main clause.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"simple-compound-and-complex-sentences-3-1-3-3\">Simple, compound and complex sentences (3.1, 3.3)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Made of</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>simple</td>\n<td>one independent clause: one subject, one verb with tense</td>\n<td><em>Everyone needs a coach.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>compound</td>\n<td>two or more independent clauses joined by <em>and, or, but</em> or commas</td>\n<td><em>I came, I saw, I conquered.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>complex</td>\n<td>one independent clause + one or more subordinate clauses</td>\n<td><em>We all need people <strong>who will give us feedback</strong>.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Length does not decide the type. <em>All the discoveries and inventions of the 20th century, …, have not made human beings happier</em> is long but simple; so is <em>Circling the airport for a long time in the sky, the plane was finally allowed to land</em>, because <em>circling</em> has no tense (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rse0Rxh1SlM&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w03/building-blocks-of-a-sentence\">building blocks of a sentence</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w03/a-speech-and-a-novel\">a speech and a novel</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w03/independent-clauses-and-simple-sentences\">simple sentences</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w03/compound-sentences\">compound sentences</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"three-kinds-of-subordinate-clause-3-1\">Three kinds of subordinate clause (3.1)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Its job</th>\n<th>Lecture example (the clause in bold)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>noun</strong> clause</td>\n<td>subject, object, or apposition (more information about a noun)</td>\n<td><em>He still believed <strong>that his Albert Mission set was intact</strong>.</em> (object) · <em><strong>That the pandemic would disappear</strong> was believed last year by everyone.</em> (subject)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>adjectival</strong> clause</td>\n<td>qualifies (describes) a noun</td>\n<td><em>He excited the curiosity <strong>that all newcomers do</strong>.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>adverbial</strong> clause</td>\n<td>gives additional information</td>\n<td><em><strong>While the teacher was scrutinising the sums</strong>, Swaminathan was gazing on his face.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Clause analysis in three steps (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=1275s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:15</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Count</strong> the clauses: one verb with tense = one clause.</li>\n<li>Name their <strong>nature</strong>: noun, adjectival or adverbial.</li>\n<li>State their <strong>function</strong>: “x qualifies y”, the subject or object of z, or it adds information.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two subordinate clauses with two jobs (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>While the teacher was scrutinising the sums, Swaminathan was gazing on his face, which seemed so tame at close quarters.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Verbs with tense: <em>was scrutinising</em>, <em>was gazing</em>, <em>seemed</em>. So three clauses.</li>\n<li>Main clause: <em>Swaminathan was gazing on his face</em>.</li>\n<li><em>While the teacher was scrutinising the sums</em> only adds when it happened: <strong>adverbial</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>which seemed so tame at close quarters</em> describes the noun <em>face</em>: <strong>adjectival</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> one main clause carrying an adverbial clause and an adjectival clause. One sentence can hold subordinate clauses with different jobs.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Ask the question</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Find the word the clause hangs on, and ask: believed <strong>what</strong>? → a noun clause. <strong>Which</strong> curiosity? → an adjectival clause. <strong>When</strong>? → an adverbial clause.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w03/a-long-sentence-taken-apart\">a long sentence taken apart</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w03/three-kinds-of-subordinate-clause\">three kinds</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w03/the-three-jobs-of-a-noun-clause\">the jobs of a noun clause</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w03/clause-analysis-in-three-steps\">clause analysis</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"finite-and-non-finite-clauses-3-2\">Finite and non-finite clauses (3.2)</h3>\n<p>Four sentences about Raju differ only in what fills the object slot (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Raju likes <strong>flowers</strong>.</em> (a noun)</li>\n<li><em>Raju knows <strong>that Sarita loves flowers</strong>.</em> (a whole sentence)</li>\n<li><em>Raju thinks <strong>that Sarita knows that Seema likes Mohan</strong>.</em> (a sentence inside a sentence inside a sentence)</li>\n<li><em>Raju likes <strong>to eat pizza</strong> with his friends in the garden.</em> (a sentence without tense)</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Finite and non-finite clauses (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>finite</strong> clause has a verb with tense and agreement (<em>Sarita <strong>loves</strong> flowers</em>), and it could also stand alone. A <strong>non-finite</strong> clause has none (<em>to eat pizza</em>, an <strong>infinitival</strong> clause), so it must stay embedded: ✗ <em>Sarita to eat pizza.</em> Its hidden subject is dropped when it is the same person as the main subject (<em>Raju wants to go home</em>) and said when it is a different person (<em>Raju wants <strong>Sarita</strong> to go home</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">A complex sentence is a simple sentence in disguise</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Each Raju sentence is still subject + verb + object; only the object slot holds a clause. That is why the mind has no trouble with long sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>): for the mind, “a sentence is a sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Clauses can also fill the subject slot (<em>[For Raju to get a good night sleep] is a bliss</em>), describe a noun (<em>Raju [who lives in Chennai] is a teacher of English</em>) or add information to the whole sentence (<em>[As far as rain is concerned], it is likely to begin in July</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w03/finite-and-non-finite-clauses\">finite and non-finite clauses</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w03/what-a-clause-can-do-in-a-sentence\">what a clause can do</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-adjectival-relative-clause-3-4\">The adjectival (relative) clause (3.4)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adjectival clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A clause that can stand for an adjective. It modifies a noun in any position, it can be replaced by an adjective, and it comes to the <strong>right</strong> of its noun (an adjective comes to the left). It usually begins with a <strong>relative pronoun</strong>: <em>who, which, that</em> (also <em>where, when</em>). There these words do not ask questions; they refer back to the noun.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">His “room” (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>By eight he was at his desk in his ‘room’, which was only a corner in his father’s dressing-room.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Main clause: <em>By eight he was at his desk in his room</em>.</li>\n<li><em>which was only a corner in his father’s dressing-room</em> has a verb with tense (<em>was</em>) and describes <em>room</em>.</li>\n<li>Replace it with one adjective: <em>in his <strong>corner</strong> room</em>. The adjective goes to the left of the noun; the clause stood on its right.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> an adjectival clause qualifying <em>room</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A prepositional phrase after a noun (<em>his room <strong>by the entrance</strong></em>) works like an adjective, but it is neither a clause nor an adjective: “they are adjuncts”. And <em>that</em> has several jobs: <em>that book</em> (pointing), <em>the four <strong>that</strong> he liked</em> (a relative pronoun), <em>Raju knows <strong>that</strong> Sarita loves flowers</em> (it begins a noun clause).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w03/the-adjectival-relative-clause\">the adjectival clause</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w03/one-main-clause-two-different-subordinate-clauses\">two different subordinate clauses</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w03/the-lecture-s-activity\">the activity</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"complements-and-adjuncts-3-5\">Complements and adjuncts (3.5)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Complement and adjunct (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>complement</strong> is a required, structurally <strong>indispensable</strong> part: remove it and the sentence breaks. Objects are complements: <em>John loves <strong>Mary</strong></em>.</li>\n<li>An <strong>adjunct</strong> is an optional, <strong>dispensable</strong> part: remove it and the sentence is still complete. Adverbs and most prepositional phrases are adjuncts: <em>Raju helped Ramu <strong>in the morning</strong></em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Think of an online form, with required fields (marked with a star) and optional ones (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). English is <strong>verb-medial</strong> (subject + verb + object: <em>John loves Mary</em>); Tamil, Telugu, Hindi and many other Indian languages are <strong>verb-final</strong> (subject + object + verb). Subject and verb are bound by <strong>agreement</strong> in number and person: <em>John <strong>loves</strong></em>, <em>John and Mary <strong>like</strong></em>. An <strong>imperative</strong> (an order or a request) does not say its subject <em>you</em>: <em>(You) sit down</em>. This is the <strong>principle of economy</strong>: what is obvious is not said.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Complement</th>\n<th>Adjunct</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>required?</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>who decides</td>\n<td>the head (the verb’s type)</td>\n<td>not the head</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>how many</td>\n<td>one per phrase; at most two per sentence</td>\n<td>as many as you like</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>position</td>\n<td>next to its head</td>\n<td>after the complement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>lecture example</td>\n<td><em>the King <strong>of England</strong></em></td>\n<td><em>the King of England <strong>with his ministers</strong></em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The <strong>head</strong> is the main word of a phrase, here <em>King</em>. So ✗ <em>the King with his ministers of England</em> (an adjunct between the head and its complement) and ✗ <em>the King of England of France</em> (two complements for one head).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Four tests</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>What-test</strong>: ask “verb + what?”; the answer is a complement (<em>Raju needs <strong>what</strong>? a pen</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Delete test</strong>: remove the part. If the rest breaks, it was a complement.</li>\n<li><strong>Swap test</strong>: a complement cannot move away from its head (✗ <em>a student with long hair of physics</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Count test</strong>: one head takes one complement.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w03/word-order-and-agreement\">word order and agreement</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w03/imperatives-have-a-hidden-subject\">imperatives</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w03/testing-for-complements-take-it-out\">testing for complements</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w03/testing-for-adjuncts-take-it-out\">testing for adjuncts</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w03/an-adjunct-of-the-verb-or-of-a-noun\">an adjunct of the verb or of a noun</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w03/complements-and-adjuncts-inside-a-phrase\">inside a phrase</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 3</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Long is not complex. And <em>and</em> makes a compound sentence only when it joins two <strong>independent</strong> clauses.</li>\n<li>A non-finite clause cannot stand alone: ✗ <em>Sarita to eat pizza.</em></li>\n<li>At the start of an adjectival clause, <em>who, which</em> and <em>that</em> are relative pronouns, not question words.</li>\n<li>A phrase after a noun (<em>by the entrance</em>) is an adjunct, not an adjectival clause.</li>\n<li>A <em>who</em>-clause between commas (<em>Rabindranath Tagore, who is a Nobel Laureate, …</em>) is called a “noun clause in apposition” on a 3.1 slide but an adjectival (relative) clause in 3.2 and 3.4. Either way, it only adds information about a noun that is already clear.</li>\n<li>On the 3.5 slides, “Adj” means <strong>adjunct</strong>, not adjective.</li>\n<li>An adjunct between a head and its complement: ✗ <em>a student with long hair of physics</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w03\">Week 3</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-4-writing-to-argue-instruct-and-influence-reflective-es\">Week 4: Writing to argue, instruct and influence; reflective essays</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Every purpose of writing has its own words and its own structure. <strong>Arguing</strong> presents two sides of a disputed question. A <strong>lab manual</strong> leaves nothing to guesswork. <strong>Writing to influence</strong> pushes the reader with vague words and impressive numbers. A <strong>reflective essay</strong> thinks through a question with no single answer, in 30 minutes, from a plan.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"writing-to-argue-4-1\">Writing to argue (4.1)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Writing to argue</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Writing that presents <strong>two or more sides</strong> of a disputed question, usually a <strong>policy</strong> (a plan of action of a public body), with reasons and evidence for each side. “Policies are rarely undisputed. There always are two sides, or even more” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s question: should big companies sell our daily groceries, or should that trade be left to local shops? <strong>Content</strong> (what you have to say) comes first. Note what you already know; look at local examples (the old woman who sold guavas every morning: is she still there?); search for statistics and <strong>anecdotes</strong> (short true stories); think of related cases (big companies already sell clothes, meat and milk) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</p>\n<p>The language of argument comes in five kinds (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=774s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:54</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Lecture examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>expressions that weigh sides and views</td>\n<td><em>on one hand … the other, as far as, in my view, in view of, on considering</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>adjectives of comparison</td>\n<td><em>the <strong>least</strong> polluting option</em>, <em>the <strong>most</strong> easily available option</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>adverbs that press a preference</td>\n<td><em>actually</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nouns for groups</td>\n<td><em><strong>majority</strong> groups</em>, <em><strong>half the population</strong> in metro cities</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>expressions demanding consideration</td>\n<td><em>before you conclude, while we are at it, one can as well see, in totality</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Taking apart an argument sentence (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=le0gtMHfD0A&amp;t=837s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:57</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Mass rapid transport systems are not only the most cost effective options, but actually that is the only future of big cities.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>most</em> raises <em>cost effective</em> to the top degree of comparison.</li>\n<li><em>actually</em> is an adverb that presses the writer’s preference.</li>\n<li><em>the only future</em> closes every other door.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> without these words it is a plain statement: <em>Mass rapid transport systems are cost effective options for big cities.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w04/where-the-content-comes-from\">where content comes from</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/two-sides-or-more\">two sides, or more</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/the-language-of-argument\">the language of argument</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/finding-argument-words-in-a-report\">argument words in a report</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/the-lecture-s-essay-children-computers-and-cellphones\">the essay on children and screens</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-structure-of-an-argument-4-2\">The structure of an argument (4.2)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The usual four-part structure (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=106s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:46</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>a statement of the <strong>situation</strong>;</li>\n<li>arguments for <strong>one side</strong>;</li>\n<li>in the third or fourth paragraph, arguments for the <strong>other side</strong>;</li>\n<li>an <strong>overview</strong> and an <strong>acceptable conclusion</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>Your main point may come first, last, or after the evidence: the choice is yours, but “a structure has to be there”, and it must keep the reader interested (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Statistical</strong> evidence suits policy levels; <strong>anecdotal</strong> evidence suits friends and family. Use “a judicious mix”, “like salt in sambar” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Argument sentences are often complex: <em>If … then</em>, <em>While …</em>, <em>Whereas …</em>, <em>As you sow, so you reap</em>. Even two simple clauses argue when they contrast: <em>Stars twinkle, planets do not</em> (Nehru, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-Aunqsk8w0o&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>The story test</strong>: words like <em>set aside, as presented here, completely ignores, moreover, at least</em> “would not be there” if the text told a story.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both model essays follow the four parts, and contrast words mark each turn:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>Children and screens (4.1)</th>\n<th>Big chain stores (4.2)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. situation</td>\n<td>screens are part of life, but access for children is “a contested notion”</td>\n<td>globalisation changed trade; is “such sea change” welcome?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. one side</td>\n<td>unrestricted access can harm the child</td>\n<td>every link of the market gains</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. the other side</td>\n<td><em><strong>However</strong>, computer skills are essential</em></td>\n<td><em><strong>While</strong> everyone … may benefit</em>, outsiders “may stand to lose much”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. conclusion</td>\n<td><em><strong>Although</strong> …</em>, restricted access is necessary</td>\n<td><em><strong>However</strong></em>, let them in “as long as the risks are averted”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w04/where-to-put-your-main-point\">where to put your main point</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/the-usual-four-part-structure\">the four-part structure</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/evidence-statistics-or-anecdotes\">evidence</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/sentences-of-argument\">sentences of argument</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/two-extracts-on-working-parents\">two extracts</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/the-lecture-s-model-essay-big-chain-stores\">the model essay</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-lab-manual-4-3\">A lab manual (4.3)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Lab manual</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>manual</strong> tells a reader, step by step, how to do a task, like a recipe. It demands “greatest possible accuracy in terms of words for (a) things and actions, i.e. nouns and verbs, … (b) sequence of their use, (c) duration of their use, (d) changes that may occur” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=252s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:12</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Actions</strong> are not interchangeable: <em>boil, steam, simmer, fry, bake, roast</em>. <strong>Sequence</strong>: “If you fry something before it is boiled, it will be wasted.” <strong>Duration</strong>: <em>until it boils, until it is lukewarm</em>. <strong>Changes</strong>: form, smell, colour, taste.</li>\n<li>Experts go wrong by skipping “obvious” steps: “cut it, fry it” hides at least seven steps (wash, wipe, put it on the board, hold it, cut equal slices, oil in the pan first, do not let it burn). “Accidents happen when you are either in a hurry or careless” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Checking the lecture’s coffee manual against the four kinds of words (the model at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F0CnZHLSyYs&amp;t=1284s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:24</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Things</strong>: a pan or kettle, a stove, a coffee filter, a coffee mug; water, coffee powder.</li>\n<li><strong>Actions</strong>: take, pour, place, light, attach, add, press, close, detach.</li>\n<li><strong>Sequence</strong>: <em>meanwhile</em>; <em>once</em> the bubbles settle; <em>immediately</em>; <em>after</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Duration</strong>: <em>until</em> the water boils; <em>till</em> it reaches a little below the rim; <em>after 5 minutes</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Changes</strong>: the bubbles settle; the decoction (the strong coffee liquid) collects in the bottom part.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> every kind of word is there, so a beginner can follow the manual without guessing.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w04/four-kinds-of-exact-words\">four kinds of exact words</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/why-experts-make-mistakes\">why experts make mistakes</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/the-lecture-s-manual-making-tea\">making tea</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/the-lecture-s-model-preparing-coffee\">preparing coffee</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"writing-to-influence-4-4\">Writing to influence (4.4)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Writing to influence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Writing that tries to make readers think and believe as the writer does: the daily work of advertising, <strong>propaganda</strong> (pushing one view on many people) and <strong>rhetoric</strong> (the art of persuading). Influencing is not unethical when you believe what you write; the lecture studies the <strong>language</strong> people use for it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Its marks: words that hide quantity (<em>recent weeks, numerous, several, a strong preference, continuing to rise</em>: How recent? How many? “Can we quantify strong?”); percentages without the number of people behind them; a small sample described in big words (“over 400 middle and high-income groups in Delhi and surrounding NCR cities”); tentative words (<em>preference, in the short run</em>); and even “we are the best”.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">How many people is “78.1%”? (the lecture’s survey at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fd70FMSa9rg&amp;t=730s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:10</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The survey asked 350 people. Private vehicles 78.1%, cabs 8.7%, buses or metro 7.2%, and “the remaining voted for autos”.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">78.1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8.7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7.2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">94.0</span></span></span></span>,</span> so autos are <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">94.0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8056em;vertical-align:-0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6.0%</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>Private vehicles: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.781</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">350</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">273.35</span></span></span></span>,</span> about 273 people.</li>\n<li>Cabs: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.087</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">350</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">30.45</span></span></span></span>,</span> about 30. Buses or metro: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.072</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">350</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25.2</span></span></span></span>,</span> about 25. Autos: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.06</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">350</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">21</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>Check: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">273.35</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">30.45</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25.2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">21</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">350</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the impressive “78.1%” is about 273 people. Always ask: <strong>of how many?</strong></p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w04/is-it-right-to-influence\">is it right to influence?</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/words-that-hide-quantity\">words that hide quantity</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/percentages-without-people-the-surveys\">the surveys</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/the-practice-report-and-essay\">the practice report and essay</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reflective-essays-4-5-4-6\">Reflective essays (4.5, 4.6)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Reflective essay and synopsis</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>reflective essay</strong> is on a topic with no single right answer, such as the lecture’s “One global culture will create global happiness. Do you agree?” Do not start writing at once: first make a <strong>synopsis</strong>, “an overview, like you stand on the rooftop and look at the entire city” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=768s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:48</a>). Write it as hints, with only relevant points, covering <strong>all</strong> parts of the topic. “Once we make a synopsis, half the job is done.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>The lecture’s synopsis: <em>Definition of happiness – instances of happiness – empathy – cut-throat competition – work life – statistical evidence – working hours – English language for job application – number of suicides – mental and physical health</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=526s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:46</a>).</li>\n<li>A <strong>partisan</strong> synopsis covers one side only. For “Should one have unlimited freedom? Should there be no censorship for mass media?”, Synopsis-1 lists media and censorship and says nothing about the individual’s freedom; the better one adds absolute and relative freedom (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cE0ZsSI9UKk&amp;t=1281s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:21</a>).</li>\n<li>Give about 20–25% of the words to stories and instances from your own life: they are the one thing other writers do not have (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=372s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:12</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Cohesion</strong> (connectedness): link sentences with connectors (<em>however, therefore, thus, that is why</em>), correct prepositions (<em>the basic need <strong>of</strong> every human being</em>) and pronouns after their nouns (<em>Saints have said … <strong>They</strong> have also said …</em>), like mortar between bricks (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=796s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:16</a>).</li>\n<li>Write several <strong>drafts</strong>, then cut to the word limit: remove redundant words, use one word for many, rewrite long sentences. The lecture’s Draft 2 had 444 words for a 200-word task: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">444</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">244</span></span></span></span></span> words had to go.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A 200-word essay in 30 minutes (the lecture’s plan at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5QlR0QUXeAQ&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Synopsis 5 minutes + writing 20 minutes + revision 5 minutes: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">30</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>Planning and revising take <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">30</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">10</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span> of the time; writing takes <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>Speed: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span></span></span></span></span> words a minute.</li>\n<li>Stories, 20–25% of the words: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">40</span></span></span></span></span> to <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.25</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">50</span></span></span></span></span> words.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 5 + 20 + 5 minutes, 10 words a minute, and 40 to 50 words of stories.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w04/do-not-start-writing-at-once\">do not start at once</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/what-a-synopsis-is\">what a synopsis is</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/the-lecture-s-synopsis-on-happiness\">the happiness synopsis</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/why-the-five-minutes-are-worth-it\">the five minutes</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/a-partisan-synopsis-and-a-better-one\">a partisan synopsis</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/plan-your-30-minutes\">30 minutes</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/stories-make-your-essay-different\">stories</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/cohesion-the-glue-of-an-essay\">cohesion</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/drafts-the-happiness-essay\">drafts</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w04/cutting-to-the-word-limit\">cutting</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 4</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A one-sided (partisan) essay: check that every part of the topic has a point in your synopsis.</li>\n<li>Trusting group words and percentages: “half the population” and “78.1%” mean little until you ask “of how many?”</li>\n<li>A manual that skips “obvious” steps (“cut it, fry it”).</li>\n<li>Starting to write at once, without a synopsis.</li>\n<li>A <em>while</em>-clause alone is not a sentence: ✗ <em>While some may consider being close to nature gives them happiness.</em></li>\n<li>Unchecked facts: “the pursuit of happiness” comes from the American Declaration of Independence, not the Constitution.</li>\n<li>Cutting by mindless deletion: rewrite, and keep the meaning.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w04\">Week 4</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-5-mood-and-modal-verbs\">Week 5: Mood and modal verbs</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Raju likes pizza</em> reports a <strong>fact</strong>. <em>Raju can eat pizza</em> does not even say that he eats it: <em>can</em> adds the speaker’s view of what is possible. Such helping words are <strong>modal auxiliaries</strong>. Week 5 explains what they are, and how to choose between <em>can</em> and <em>could</em>, <em>will</em> and <em>would</em>, <em>could</em> and <em>would</em>, <em>must</em>, <em>shall</em> and <em>should</em>, and <em>may</em> and <em>might</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"modal-auxiliaries-and-mood-5-1\">Modal auxiliaries and mood (5.1)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Tense auxiliaries and modal auxiliaries</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>auxiliary</strong> is a helping word before a main verb. <strong>Tense auxiliaries</strong> carry tense: <em>is, am, are</em> (present), <em>was, were</em> (past), <em>shall, will</em> (future). <strong>Modal auxiliaries</strong> (<strong>modals</strong>) are <em>can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will, would</em>, and they carry <strong>no tense</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). Lectures 5.2–5.4 call them <strong>modal verbs</strong>, the common name; 5.1 insists that modals are auxiliaries, not verbs.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Set 1</th>\n<th>Set 2</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>can</td>\n<td>could</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>may</td>\n<td>might</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>must</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>shall</td>\n<td>should</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>will</td>\n<td>would</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Set 2 is <strong>not</strong> the past of Set 1: each is an independent modal. The lecturer’s proof: <em>shall</em> and <em>will</em> mark the future, and a future marker cannot have a past form (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Modality and mood</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Modality</strong> is the speaker’s comment on necessity or possibility: a modal sentence is <strong>non-factual</strong>. Its <strong>readings</strong> (the meanings it can give) are possibility, ability, necessity, certainty, obligation and willingness (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=640s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:40</a>). Adverbs (<em>perhaps</em>) and <strong>hedges</strong> (<em>I would think …</em>, <em>If I were you …</em>) express it too.</li>\n<li><strong>Mood</strong> is modality coded in grammar. The <strong>imperative</strong> asks: <em>Help me with these issues.</em> The <strong>subjunctive</strong> (or <strong>optative</strong>) wishes: <em>May you live long!</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>One modal can have several readings: <em>Anyone <strong>can</strong> become a minister</em> (possibility); <em><strong>Can</strong> I ask you a question?</em> (permission); <em>She <strong>can</strong> walk fast</em> (ability).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Negation shows where the tense is (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zpXHE-M5nco&amp;t=1030s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:10</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>Raju <strong>likes</strong> pizza</em>: the present tense and the <em>-s</em> sit on the verb.</li>\n<li>Negative: <em>Raju <strong>does not</strong> like pizza</em>. The tense moves to <em>does</em>, and the verb is left bare.</li>\n<li>Past: <em>Raju liked pizza</em> → <em>Raju <strong>did not</strong> like pizza</em>.</li>\n<li>With a modal: <em>Raju <strong>can</strong> eat pizza</em> → <em>Raju <strong>cannot</strong> eat pizza</em>. No <em>does</em> appears, because <em>can</em> has no tense to give away.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> a modal needs no <em>do, does</em> or <em>did</em> in negatives and questions (<em>Can you eat pizza?</em>), takes no <em>-s</em>, <em>-ing</em> or <em>to</em> (✗ <em>she cans</em>, ✗ <em>to can</em>), and is followed by the bare verb (✗ <em>Raju can ate pizza</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>To talk about the past, use modal + <em>have</em> + V3: <em>It <strong>must have been</strong> a difficult decision.</em> · <em>You <strong>should have warned</strong> me earlier.</em> The past time comes from <em>have</em> + V3, not from the modal.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w05/two-sets-of-modals\">two sets of modals</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w05/modality-and-mood\">modality and mood</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w05/the-readings-of-a-modal\">the readings of a modal</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w05/how-a-modal-behaves-in-a-sentence\">how a modal behaves</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"which-modal-when-5-2-5-6\">Which modal when (5.2–5.6)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>To express</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ability now / in the past</td>\n<td><em>can</em> / <em>could</em></td>\n<td><em>I could play tennis when I was in school.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>possibility in a situation</td>\n<td><em>could</em>; <em>may</em>; <em>might</em> (the weakest)</td>\n<td><em>The bus could be late.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>general possibility</td>\n<td><em>can</em></td>\n<td><em>Living in New York can cost a fortune.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>suggestion</td>\n<td><em>could</em></td>\n<td><em>We could try Thai food this time.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>offer</td>\n<td><em>Would you like …?</em>; <em>Can/Could I …?</em></td>\n<td><em>Would you like some coffee?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>request</td>\n<td><em>can, will</em> (informal); <em>could, would</em> (formal)</td>\n<td><em>Could you send me the details by email, please?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>permission</td>\n<td><em>can</em> (informal); <em>could, may</em> (formal)</td>\n<td><em>May I take a tea break?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>imaginary situation</td>\n<td><em>would</em></td>\n<td><em>If I had a lot of money, I would retire early.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>future fact or guess</td>\n<td><em>will</em></td>\n<td><em>Don’t lend him your car. He’ll crash it.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>past habit</td>\n<td><em>would</em> (action verbs); <em>used to</em> (state verbs)</td>\n<td><em>When I was young, I used to live in an old house.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>deduction</td>\n<td><em>must</em></td>\n<td><em>The fan is not working. It must be power failure.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>obligation</td>\n<td><em>must</em>; <em>shall</em> (strong, legal); <em>should</em> (weak: advice)</td>\n<td><em>You should inform me before you leave.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>denial</td>\n<td><em>why</em> + <em>should</em></td>\n<td><em>Why should I do what everyone else does?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>probability</td>\n<td><em>should</em></td>\n<td><em>This work should take two or three days.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>concession</td>\n<td><em>may / might …, but …</em></td>\n<td><em>He may be a child, but he is very wise.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Can and could (5.2).</strong> <em>Could</em> is the past of ability and the more polite word: with <em>please</em> or in a formal setting, prefer it. For possibility in one particular situation, only <em>could</em> works: <em>It <strong>could</strong> be grandpa</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qglDbdAnzkY&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Will and would (5.3).</strong> <em>Will</em> is for <strong>real</strong> situations and always points to the <strong>future</strong>: information (<em>We’ll head home after work</em>), guesses, willingness (<em>I will eat anything</em>), rules (<em>Smokers will be asked to smoke outside</em>) and orders (<em>You will tidy up your room</em>). <em>Would</em> is for <strong>imagined</strong> situations, the past, and politeness: past expectations (<em>…which meant it would rain</em>), past habits (<em>When we lived in the mountains, we would go hiking all the time</em>), reported speech (<em>She told me that she would be here at 8 o’clock</em>), formal requests (<em>Would you please come in?</em>), likes (<em>I would love to go on a trip to Spain</em>), opinions on uncertain things (<em>I would say he is about forty</em>), refusals (<em>Reva wouldn’t go to the doctor</em>), after <em>wish</em> (<em>I wish you would be quiet for a minute</em>) and results (<em>so that his mother would never read them</em>). Conditionals: zero (no modal), first (<em>will</em>), second (<em>would</em>), third (<em>would have</em> + V3); the full table is in <a href=\"#/english2/w10/all-the-types-together\">Week 10</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Could and would (5.4).</strong> For a <strong>possible</strong> situation use <em>could</em>, never <em>would</em>: <em>Miss Maya has set the question paper. The exam could be very tough.</em> For an <strong>imaginary</strong> one, prefer <em>would</em>: <em>If Kishore worked hard, he would pass the exam.</em> Suggestions take <em>could</em>; offers take <em>would</em> (✗ <em>Could you like some tea?</em>); requests take either; <em>Would you mind …?</em> is a fixed phrase (✗ <em>Could you mind …?</em>); permission takes <em>could</em> (✗ <em>Would I borrow your pen?</em>) or <em>Would it be OK if I <strong>borrowed</strong> your bike …?</em>, with a past verb after it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=meJgUe5W6Yg&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Must, shall and should (5.5).</strong> <em>Must</em> means <strong>necessity</strong> from a deduction (<em>Look at how thick that canopy is. The trees must have many branches.</em>) or <strong>obligation</strong>: self-imposed and weak (<em>I must clean the room</em>), non-personal rules (<em>Submissions must follow the guidelines as instructed by the scientific committee</em>) or social (<em>We must visit Ramesh’s uncle and his family</em>). <em>Mustn’t</em> is forceful: <em>You mustn’t use your phones while at a fuel station.</em> <em>Shall</em> with <em>I</em> or <em>we</em> shows <strong>determination</strong>, and also intention, willingness (<em>Shall we go?</em>) and prediction; in legal language it is a <strong>strong obligation</strong> (<em>The petitioner shall present an affidavit …</em>). <em>Should</em> means <strong>requirement</strong>, a weak obligation: advice (<em>You should prepare well for the learner’s test</em>), denial in <em>why</em>-questions, and probability (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kRKZiO9QyZg&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>May and might (5.6).</strong> <em>May</em> gives a possibility with an equal chance either way (<em>The delivery may be delayed</em>: in practice, a polite way to give bad news); formal permission (<em>May I …?</em> shows dependence on someone with authority, while <em>Can I …?</em> is casual, because <em>can</em> carries a sense of ability); and <strong>concession</strong>, accepting a point that does not change the main one (<em>The school may be open on Sundays, but the children will not go</em>). <em>Might</em> has the same readings but is <strong>remote</strong>: a weaker possibility (<em>The Vice-Chancellor might come tomorrow</em>), a hypothetical one (<em>She is a bright student, so she might become successful in life</em>), a less relevant concession. It is not a past tense (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-PACFxfrcgc&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two kinds of offer</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Can/Could <strong>I</strong> get you something to drink?</em> offers to <strong>do</strong> something. <em>Would <strong>you</strong> like some coffee?</em> asks what the other person <strong>wants</strong>. <em>Could you like …?</em> is always wrong.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w05/can-and-could\">can and could</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w05/will-and-would\">will and would</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w05/conditionals\">conditionals</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w05/could-and-would\">could and would</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w05/must-shall-and-should\">must, shall and should</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w05/may-and-might\">may and might</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 5</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Could, might, should, would</em> are not the past tenses of <em>can, may, shall, will</em>.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>Raju can ate pizza</em>; ✗ <em>she cans</em>; ✗ <em>to can</em>.</li>\n<li>A possibility takes <em>could</em>: ✗ <em>The exam would be very tough.</em> ✗ <em>He would be busy.</em></li>\n<li>✗ <em>Could you like some tea?</em> ✗ <em>Could you mind …?</em> ✗ <em>Would I borrow your pen?</em></li>\n<li>A second conditional takes <em>would</em>: ✗ <em>If I had a magic wand, I will change some historical events.</em></li>\n<li><em>Would</em> for a past habit only with action verbs: ✗ <em>When I was young, I would live in an old house</em> → <em>used to live</em>.</li>\n<li>An opinion on something uncertain: ✗ <em>I will say he is about forty</em> → <em>I would say</em>.</li>\n<li><em>This work must take two or three days</em> is grammatical but wrong for the context: use <em>should</em> for probability.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w05\">Week 5</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-6-softening-question-tags-questions-and-embedded-clause\">Week 6: Softening, question tags, questions and embedded clauses</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Week 6 has two halves. Lecture 6.1 is about <strong>using</strong> language with care: saying things gently, so that they suit the listener and the context. Lectures 6.2–6.6 show the machinery behind negatives, tags and questions: the <strong>tense</strong> moves out of the verb and to the front, except inside a bigger sentence, where nothing moves.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"softening-6-1\">Softening (6.1)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Accuracy, appropriateness and softening</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Accuracy</strong> (or <strong>grammaticality</strong>) is following the rules of grammar. <strong>Appropriateness</strong> is being right for the listener and the context. Together they make us <strong>impactful</strong>. <strong>Softening</strong> is saying things gently, so that the listener stays with us. Grammar comes first: be “grammatically accurate plus communicatively appropriate”, and the second part “comes only after the first one” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>). There is no fixed rule: the context decides.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Harsh (grammatical, but abrupt)</th>\n<th>Softer (the lecture’s slides)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Meet with me at 9 in the morning.</em></td>\n<td><em>Could we meet at 9 in the morning, please?</em> · <em>I was wondering if we could meet at 9 in the morning.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>No, you should not.</em> (to <em>Shall I invest in mutual funds?</em>)</td>\n<td><em>If I were you, I would think several times about it.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>You have made a mistake in calculations.</em></td>\n<td><em>You seem to have missed a few things. May I suggest a revisit?</em> · <em>It appears to me that the calculations do not add up. Could you please take a look at it again?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>No, I cannot go for a cup of tea.</em></td>\n<td><em>I have a lot of stuff pending to take care of. Hope you won’t mind.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>No, I cannot bring them.</em> (storybooks)</td>\n<td><em>I will try my best.</em> · <em>I am not sure if there is any bookstore on my way.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Please</em> alone does not make a request. From an army general to his lieutenants, <em>Please meet with me at 9 in the morning</em> is still a <strong>command</strong>; in a civil office, you could suggest <em>Sir, is nine-thirty okay?</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</li>\n<li>The three softer requests go from polite (<em>Could we …?</em>) to more polite (<em>…, please?</em>) to “excessively polite” (<em>I was wondering if …</em>): choose by context.</li>\n<li>The soft suggestion “does not impose” on the hearer. A skilful refusal says no “without having said no”: “these are not the ways of lying, but these are ways of communication” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2460s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 41:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w06/formal-and-informal-command-or-request\">command or request</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/softer-versions-of-a-request\">softer requests</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/suggestions\">suggestions</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/discussing-an-issue\">discussing an issue</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/refusal-saying-no\">refusal</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"negatives-and-question-tags-6-2\">Negatives and question tags (6.2)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Negatives: the tense comes out of the verb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In a negative sentence the tense leaves the verb: <strong>tense + <em>not</em> + verb</strong>. <em>I know</em> → <em>I <strong>do not</strong> know</em>; <em>Raju confirmed</em> → <em>Raju <strong>did not</strong> confirm</em>. If the tense is already outside the verb (on <em>have</em> or <em>was</em>), <em>not</em> simply follows it: <em>They have <strong>not</strong> had food</em>; <em>Raju was <strong>not</strong> going to speak with his friends.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Question tag (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>question tag</strong> is a short question at the end of a statement, after a comma. It keeps the <strong>same subject</strong> (as a pronoun) and the <strong>same tense</strong> (with agreement); it takes the <strong>opposite</strong> form (affirmative sentence → negative tag; negative sentence → positive tag); and in a negative tag, the tense and <em>not</em> <strong>must</strong> be contracted (<em>don’t, isn’t, didn’t</em>). Tags <strong>engage</strong> the listener, <strong>verify</strong> and <strong>confirm</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>You speak English, don’t you?</em> (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The sentence is affirmative, so the tag is negative.</li>\n<li>Subject: <em>you</em>. Tense: present; <em>speak</em> has no helper, so <em>do</em> carries the tense.</li>\n<li>Make it negative and contract it: <em>do</em> + <em>not</em> → <em>don’t</em>.</li>\n<li>Tense word first, then the subject: <em>don’t you?</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>You speak English, don’t you?</em> For the negative sentence: <em>You do not speak English, do you?</em> In the past: <em>You liked traveling, didn’t you?</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>More from the slides: <em>He is devastated with the outcome, <strong>isn’t he</strong>?</em> · <em>Whenever we play chess, Raju beats me, <strong>doesn’t he</strong>?</em> (the slide misprints <em>she</em>) · <em>She never misses an opportunity to make me feel bad, <strong>does she</strong>?</em> (<em>never</em> makes the sentence negative) · <em>He will not allow this, <strong>will he</strong>?</em> Tags such as <em>right?</em> and <em>isn’t it?</em> come from our first languages: people understand them, but the appropriate tag is <em>don’t you?</em></p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w06/affirmative-and-negative-sentences\">affirmative and negative</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/building-the-tag\">building the tag</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/why-we-use-tags\">why tags</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/the-influence-of-the-first-language\">the first language</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/the-lecture-s-examples\">the lecture’s examples</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"questions-6-3\">Questions (6.3)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The rule of questions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Tense plus agreement gets fronted to form the question.” <strong>Agreement</strong> is the match between the subject’s person and number and the verb (<em>Ramu love<strong>s</strong></em>). If the sentence has a tense word (<em>is, was</em>), that word moves to the front. If the tense is on the verb, <em>do</em> carries it: <em>does</em> = <em>do</em> + present, <em>did</em> = <em>do</em> + past.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Statement</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Raju loves pizza.</em></td>\n<td><em>What <strong>does</strong> Raju love?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Raju ate thin pizza in the evening.</em></td>\n<td><em>What <strong>did</strong> Raju eat in the evening?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Raju is coming to Chennai tomorrow.</em></td>\n<td><em><strong>Is</strong> Raju coming to Chennai tomorrow?</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A <strong>content</strong> (wh-) question needs information as its answer (<em>What does Raju love? Pizza.</em>); a <strong>yes-or-no</strong> question begins with the tense word (<em>Was Raju coming to Chennai?</em>). In speech, ask; do not <strong>interrogate</strong> (question someone hard, like an investigator) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w06/making-a-question\">making a question</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/two-types-of-questions\">two types of questions</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"embedded-questions-and-clauses-6-4-6-6\">Embedded questions and clauses (6.4–6.6)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Complex sentence and complementizer (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In Lectures 6.4 and 6.6, a sentence is <strong>complex</strong> when its predicate contains another sentence as the <strong>object of the verb</strong>: <em>I wonder [what her name is]</em>. The inner sentence is the <strong>embedded</strong> (subordinate) clause; the outer one is the <strong>matrix</strong>. A word that introduces such an object clause is a <strong>complementizer</strong>: <em>that, whether, if</em>. <em>That</em> can be dropped before a clause with its own tense: <em>I wish (that) I had a lot of money.</em> Two sentences joined by <em>and</em> (<em>Raju likes pizza and Ramu likes sandwich</em>) are <strong>coordinated</strong>, not complex.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">No fronting inside a bigger sentence (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Direct question: <em>What did you eat in the evening?</em> The past is fronted as <em>did</em>.</li>\n<li>Make it the object of <em>asked</em>: <em>I asked [ … ]</em>.</li>\n<li>Inside the brackets it is no longer a question, so nothing moves: the past stays on the verb (<em>ate</em>), in the order subject + verb (<em>you ate</em>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>I asked what you ate in the evening.</em> ✗ <em>I asked what did you eat in the evening</em> is a common mistake in Indian English.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Embedding also softens: <em>What is her name?</em> → <em>I wonder what her name is</em>; <em>Is the doctor available?</em> → <em>Could you tell me if the doctor is available?</em></p>\n<p><strong>Whether and if</strong> (6.5) begin indirect yes-or-no questions; they “add mildness”. Only <em>whether</em> works before a <em>to</em>-infinitive (<em>I’m not sure <strong>whether</strong> to get a new laptop</em>), after a preposition (<em>There are doubts about <strong>whether</strong> the decision was fair</em>) and right before <em>or not</em> (<em>The question is <strong>whether or not</strong> we have the right to interfere</em>). With <em>or</em> later in the sentence both work: <em>whether</em> is formal, <em>if</em> informal (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>That-clauses</strong> (6.6). A subject can be the unspoken <em>you</em> (<em>(You) sit down, please</em>), an <strong>expletive</strong> <em>it</em> or <em>there</em> (an empty word that only fills the subject place: <em>It is raining</em>), a noun phrase, or a whole clause: <em><strong>That Raju delivered a good lecture</strong> pleased Ramu</em> (test: <em>It pleased Ramu</em>). <em>The fact that …</em> lightens a heavy subject, and <em>It surprised me that he was still in bed</em> moves the clause to the end. An embedded clause is <strong>finite</strong>, with its own tense (<em>I believe that <strong>he is innocent</strong></em>), or <strong>non-finite</strong>, without one (<em>I want <strong>him to go</strong></em>; <em>I want ∅ to go</em>, where ∅ marks the dropped subject). A clause inside a clause inside a clause is <strong>recursion</strong>: <em>I would like [to know [what he did best]]</em>. <strong>Ditransitive</strong> verbs (<em>tell, inform, advise, assure, convince, remind, promise, teach</em>) take a person and a <em>that</em>-clause: <em>The pilot informed the passengers that the weather was not good.</em> Heavy elements go to the end: ✗ <em>He emailed to us his response</em>; ✓ <em>He emailed us his response</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=2280s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:00</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w06/that-the-complementizer\">the complementizer</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/questions-inside-a-complex-sentence\">questions inside a complex sentence</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/embedded-questions-sound-polite\">polite embedded questions</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/where-only-whether-works\">where only whether works</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/which-verbs-take-these-clauses\">which verbs</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/kinds-of-subjects\">kinds of subjects</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/kinds-of-objects\">kinds of objects</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/finite-and-non-finite-clauses\">finite and non-finite</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/which-sentences-are-complex\">which sentences are complex</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/that-clauses-as-subjects\">that-clauses as subjects</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/verbs-with-two-objects\">two objects</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w06/where-the-two-objects-go\">where they go</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 6</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Please</em> alone does not turn a command into a request; the context decides.</li>\n<li>Softening never replaces grammar.</li>\n<li>Tags must be contracted: ✗ <em>do not you?</em>, ✗ <em>is not he?</em> And ✗ <em>You speak English, isn’t it?</em> → <em>don’t you?</em></li>\n<li><em>never</em> makes the sentence negative, so its tag is positive (<em>does she?</em>).</li>\n<li>Embedded questions do not front the tense: ✗ <em>I asked what did you eat</em>; ✗ <em>I know what did you want</em>.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>I cannot decide if to accept the new job offer</em> → <em>whether to accept</em>.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>He informed that he had an urgent meeting to attend</em> (who was informed?) → <em>He informed <strong>us</strong> that …</em></li>\n<li>Lectures 6.4 and 6.6 call a sentence complex only when the embedded clause is an <strong>object</strong>; Week 3 counts any subordinate clause. Say which definition you use.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w06\">Week 6</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-7-radio-reports-numbers-interviews-and-group-discussion\">Week 7: Radio reports, numbers, interviews and group discussions</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Week 7 is speaking in practice. A radio report has only words; numbers must be said so that nobody mishears them, and shown in relation to one another; an interview is a formal conversation; a group discussion builds a team. In all of them, knowledge of the subject and preparation come first, then the language.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-radio-report-7-1\">The radio report (7.1)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Radio report</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A spoken report that the listener only <strong>hears</strong>. A radio reporter depends on words more than a newspaper or television reporter: they must “translate everything visual or verbal into words”: what happened, what was said, where, and how the place and the people looked (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Two rules for every report (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>): say everything that is possible <strong>in the given time</strong>, and give “a fair representation to <strong>all parts</strong> of the topic”. For the practice (Sunita Williams’s tour of the space station), note the names, the special words, the sequence of sights and the differences from life on earth; watch first without subtitles, then with them; report in 3, then 2½, then 2 minutes; record it, take feedback and redo it. Write first if you must, but do not lean on the script: “it takes away your naturalness” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxobbm5yhm4&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w07/what-makes-a-presentation-good\">what makes a presentation good</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/why-radio-is-the-hardest-test\">why radio is the hardest test</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/two-rules-for-every-report\">two rules</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/the-activity-sunita-williams-in-space\">Sunita Williams in space</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"presenting-numbers-7-2-7-3\">Presenting numbers (7.2, 7.3)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A misheard number can be fatal. In the lecture’s story, a pilot told to wait at 30,000 feet heard <strong>13,000</strong> and hit a plane that had just taken off (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). (The real collision near Delhi, in 1996, differs in detail; the lesson does not.)</li>\n<li>People confuse <em>13/30, 14/40, 15/50, 17/70, 19/90</em>, and drop the final <strong>v</strong> of <em>five</em> (“twenty fy”). Letters too: <em>P</em> is heard as <em>G, V, C, D</em> or <em>E</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Words that travel with numbers</strong> prepare the listener: <em>much, many, few</em>; <em>all, none, mostly, hardly ever</em>; <em>dozens, lakhs, millions, scores, quintals</em>; <em>first, last, penultimate, greater than</em>; <em>per cent, proportion, ratio</em>. A <strong>dozen</strong> is 12, a <strong>score</strong> 20, a <strong>lakh</strong> 1,00,000 (one hundred thousand), and <em>penultimate</em> means second from the last.</li>\n<li>Digits are read differently in different places (“one double zero” in India, “one zero zero” elsewhere): “you can get away with mistakes of pronunciation and grammar, but you cannot get away with mistakes of culture” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</li>\n<li>“Deliver all numbers and related words slowly and carefully.” Listeners are waiting for numbers, as at a station announcement.</li>\n<li>Do not overpack: “If you have 120 seconds, then plan for no more than 110 seconds.” With 40 seconds to accept a Nobel Prize, one witty, humble sentence beats a life story (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">-teen or -ty: the stress decides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In <em>thirty, forty, fifty …</em> the stress is on the <strong>first</strong> syllable: <strong>THIR</strong>-ty. In <em>thirteen, fourteen …</em> it is on the <strong>second</strong>, with a long <em>ee</em> (“your teeth should show, your lips should spread”): thir-<strong>TEEN</strong>. In an abbreviation read letter by letter, the <strong>last</strong> letter is stressed: U-<strong>K</strong>, U-S-<strong>A</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Read a long number with <em>and</em> before its last part: 7,965 = “seven thousand nine hundred <strong>and</strong> sixty-five”. The <em>and</em> tells the listener that the number is about to end (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Absolute and relative values (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>absolute</strong> value is a number on its own (<em>46% of primary schools</em>). A <strong>relative</strong> value shows how numbers stand to one another (<em>nearly half</em>; <em>whereas</em>; <em>out of</em>). Reading a data slide out mechanically “would not fetch you much”: show the relationships.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Percentages into words (the lecture’s model report on the education of girls at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OF4F9TFqmrg&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Find the nearest simple fraction: a quarter is 25%, a third about 33.3%, a half 50%.</li>\n<li>46% of primary schools lack basic facilities. 46 is a little <strong>below</strong> 50: “<strong>nearly half</strong>”.</li>\n<li>40% of adolescent girls are anaemic. 40 is <strong>above</strong> 33.3: “<strong>over one third</strong>”.</li>\n<li>27% marry early. 27 is <strong>above</strong> 25: “<strong>over a quarter</strong>”.</li>\n<li>8% have children early. Small and exact: “<strong>eight of a hundred</strong>”.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> below the fraction, say <em>nearly, almost, a little under</em>; above it, say <em>over, more than, a little over</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Other relative words (7.3): <em>whereas, on one hand … on the other, out of, as high as, as low as, together with, no longer</em>. In the lecture’s three villages, <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">11</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">22</span></span></span></span></span> schools, but “in yet another village … there are <strong>only</strong> three”: the relative words show the uneven spread that the total hides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). Never claim more than the data say: 5,629 undelivered messages to Chennai’s street vendors do not prove that the phones had no connectivity (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2nbouKJ0J1o&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>). For the report on a wedding in Nigeria, use sequence words (<em>first, next, then, before, after</em>), position words and words for rituals (<em>bowing, squatting, prostrating, chanting</em>): “speak like a radio reporter but see like a television camera”.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w07/a-number-misheard\">a number misheard</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/numbers-and-letters-that-sound-alike\">sound-alike numbers</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/words-that-travel-with-numbers\">words with numbers</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/reading-digits-a-matter-of-culture\">reading digits</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/listeners-are-waiting-for-numbers\">listeners wait for numbers</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/time-do-not-overpack\">do not overpack</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/the-data\">the girls’ education data</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/the-model-report\">the model report</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/teen-or-ty-the-stress-decides\">-teen or -ty</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/numbers-in-relation-to-one-another\">relative values</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/the-data-and-how-to-say-it\">the street vendors</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/the-model-answer\">the model answer</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/walking-an-extra-mile-a-wedding-in-nigeria\">a wedding in Nigeria</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"at-an-interview-7-4\">At an interview (7.4)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Interview</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“A meeting of people face to face, especially for consultation” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=125s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:05</a>): a formal conversation between people who do not know one another well. Both sides look for what the papers do not show.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The five qualities of a successful candidate, from two film interviews (<em>The Pursuit of Happyness</em> and Satyajit Ray’s <em>Pratidwandi</em>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=765s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:45</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quality</th>\n<th>In one line</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>confident</td>\n<td>you know your subject; confidence comes from knowledge and experience</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>polite</td>\n<td>respect shows in language (<em>thanks, please, sorry, pardon me</em>); stay unprovoked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a good listener</td>\n<td>total attention, no distraction, no guessing, mental notes; ask for clarification</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>honest and frank</td>\n<td>tell the truth, without being rude</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>appearance</td>\n<td>clean, comfortable, neither overdressed nor underdressed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Chris in <em>The Pursuit of Happyness</em> says that if he does not know an answer, he will say so and do his best to find it: confidence is not knowing everything. To a provocative question (“Are you a communist?”), the candidate in <em>Pratidwandi</em> answers calmly: “One does not have to be a communist to admire the people of Vietnam” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KH1OHK4Y6ao&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w07/why-interviews-happen\">why interviews happen</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/two-film-interviews\">two film interviews</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/five-qualities-of-a-successful-candidate\">five qualities</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"group-discussions-and-introductions-7-5-7-6\">Group discussions and introductions (7.5, 7.6)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Group discussion (GD)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A discussion of a topic by a group, often watched by an observer: a tool to reach <strong>consensus</strong> (an agreement the whole group accepts) and to select people. “Makes a team. Promotes good group behaviours like cooperation, exchange of ideas, formation of functional groups, learning and optimum use of resources” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:50</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Instead of (arrogant, disrespectful)</th>\n<th>Say (team spirit, respect)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Do this. Do that.</em></td>\n<td><em>Shall we do this? Should we try in this direction?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>I will do it.</em></td>\n<td><em>I can do it. Can I have a chance?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Your argument doesn’t make any sense.</em></td>\n<td><em>Sorry, we do not understand you. Can you say it differently?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Keep quiet. I will speak first.</em></td>\n<td><em>No, no, you first please, I can wait.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Even women can come.</em></td>\n<td><em>Everyone can try.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>She is bringing it.</em> (who is <em>she</em>?)</td>\n<td><em>Aishwarya is bringing it.</em> (the name first, then the pronoun)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The language of arrogance is <strong>I-centred</strong> (<em>I, my opinion, you should, shut up</em>). Avoid slang (<em>gonna, wanna</em>) and gestures such as pointing fingers. <strong>Etiquette</strong> (polite behaviour) “best shows in language”: soft words, simple words of one or two syllables, an inclusive tone (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3CRoxMLIqM&amp;t=1475s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:35</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Introductions</strong> (7.6) depend on <strong>attitude</strong>: humble and self-critical. Introduce your own group “complimentary but not exaggerated”, with a little humour (<em>he still makes mistakes in grammar, but he is wonderful in bonding people</em>). Never read out a guest’s <strong>CV</strong> (the record of education and jobs): find the <strong>unique</strong> fact, such as the governor who gave his train berth to stranded young women, or Gandhi designing the charkha (the spinning wheel). Avoid hyperbole (✗ <em>Mr Gandhi is greater than God</em>). Great introductions share research, simple language and a slow delivery (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D7kxJ__rqaM&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w07/what-goes-wrong-in-groups\">what goes wrong in groups</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/advantages-of-a-gd\">advantages of a GD</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/the-language-of-arrogance\">the language of arrogance</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/language-don-ts\">language don’ts</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/good-group-good-language\">team spirit</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/likely-places-of-error\">likely errors</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/respect-and-disrespect\">respect</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/phrases-for-a-discussion\">phrases for a discussion</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/etiquette\">etiquette</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/introducing-your-own-group\">introducing your group</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/introducing-a-guest\">introducing a guest</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/welcoming-the-audience\">welcoming the audience</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/inviting-and-answering-questions\">inviting questions</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w07/what-great-introductions-share\">great introductions</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 7</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>thirteen</em> said like <em>thirty</em>: stress thir-<strong>TEEN</strong>. And let the final <em>v</em> of <em>five</em> be heard.</li>\n<li>Reading a data slide out without relative values.</li>\n<li>Claiming more than the data say, or changing the data’s group or unit (the slide’s 6.1 million out-of-school children are children, not only girls).</li>\n<li>Packing 120 seconds of talk into 120 seconds: plan 110.</li>\n<li>Calling a question stupid, or guessing when you did not understand it. Ask instead: <em>Please pardon me, I do not understand the question. Can you tell me again?</em></li>\n<li>I-centred language, slang, or “even women can come” in a group discussion; a pronoun before the name.</li>\n<li>Reading out a guest’s CV, or praising with hyperbole.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w07\">Week 7</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-8-statement-of-purpose-pre-cis-and-lab-reports\">Week 8: Statement of purpose, précis and lab reports</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Three kinds of careful writing. A <strong>statement of purpose</strong> tells your own story in simple words. A <strong>précis</strong> says everything a passage says in one-third of its words. A <strong>lab report</strong> records what was done, how, and with what result, in impersonal language. All three reward the same skill: saying a lot in a few exact words.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-statement-of-purpose-8-1\">The statement of purpose (8.1)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Statement of purpose (SOP)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A short piece of writing sent with an application. Mark sheets show what you passed; <strong>testimonials</strong> (letters from teachers or employers) show how others see you; the <strong>SOP</strong> “tells the reader who you are”: the story behind your successes and failures, which only you know (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=39s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:39</a>). It does not replace good grades: “be an A grade, but among the A graders, your chance lies in a good SOP” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s rules (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=672s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:12</a>): tell <strong>stories</strong>; describe <strong>specific</strong> events, incidents and targets; avoid <strong>jargon</strong> (the special words of one field), <strong>cliché</strong> (a phrase used so often that it says nothing), <strong>vagueness</strong> and <strong>ambiguity</strong> (two possible readings); use simple words and simple sentences; be brief and error-free. And write <strong>an SOP</strong>: <em>a</em> or <em>an</em> goes by the first <strong>sound</strong>, and S is read “ess” (also <em>an NGO</em>, but <em>a PhD</em>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Show, do not label (the lecture’s strong SOP at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1152s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:12</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>I come from a lower-middle class Indian family. My father is a travelling salesman, selling office stationery, and my mother supervises the work of public toilet cleaners on behalf of an NGO in Chennai.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The writer never uses the word <em>poor</em>.</li>\n<li>The facts (the parents’ jobs, an uncle’s help, an inexpensive school) draw the picture.</li>\n<li>The reader puts the label on the writer, and believes it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> give facts and stories, not labels. In the same way, <em>I love hard work</em> becomes “If I am awake for 18 hours, I like to work for 14”, and <em>I have always been curious</em> becomes the child who asked her teacher “why the rainbow would not appear every day” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>A list of achievements that the CV already gives goes “from peak to peak” with no picture of “the valley which lies in between”: “a chance wasted”.</li>\n<li>One specific moment makes a story: “after seeing blue cabbage turning red in lemon sauce, I realized that it was Chemistry rather than Coding that was my calling”.</li>\n<li>An honest weak start makes success believable: a student who scored under 30 in Mathematics was among the 25 of 300 students selected by Goldman Sachs <span class=\"nwp\">(<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">300</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">25</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">12</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8056em;vertical-align:-0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8%</span></span></span></span>),</span> and among the 5 who survived the first year.</li>\n<li>Tell what you learnt from family, sports, friends and teachers, “with honesty, with frankness, with humility”: neither boast nor hide.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w08/what-each-paper-tells-the-reader\">what each paper tells</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/a-sop-or-an-sop\">a SOP or an SOP</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/vague-claims-and-the-evidence-behind-them\">vague claims</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/a-list-of-achievements-weak\">a weak list</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/a-picture-without-a-label-strong\">a strong picture</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/more-pairs-from-the-lecture\">more pairs</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/from-below-average-to-goldman-sachs-strong\">below average to Goldman Sachs</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/what-you-learnt-outside-the-classroom\">learning outside the classroom</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"simple-words-fewer-words-8-2\">Simple words, fewer words (8.2)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Simple language (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=555s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:15</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“A sentence with only one verb is a simple sentence, a one or two-syllable word with well-known meaning is a simple word. These two make a simple language.” An SOP “is a story, not a research paper”, and its readers may come from other fields.</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>Of <em>My parents were not rich / wealthy / affluent</em> and <em>I was born in a family which was not very affluent</em>, the first is the simplest: one verb, and only common words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=807s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:27</a>).</li>\n<li>Great stories are told in simple words: the opening of Sartre’s autobiography has only one uncommon word, <em>unfrocked</em>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>redundant</strong> word tells the reader nothing new (“There was a monkey; it had a tail”): <em>a <strong>valuable</strong> asset</em>, <em><strong>complete</strong> dedication</em>, <em>lead <strong>and control</strong></em>. <em>Independent researcher</em> and <em>team player</em> contradict each other; <em>English postgraduate</em> is ambiguous (English by nationality, or in English?).</li>\n<li>The lecture’s cut: <em>My seventeen years of teaching experience and the ability to lead and control various departments can surely add value to your esteemed organization</em> (23 words) → <em>I taught for seventeen years. I can work with different kinds of people.</em> (13 words). Short is not sloppy: cut “without losing clarity” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w08/write-for-every-reader\">write for every reader</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/great-writers-use-simple-words\">great writers</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/what-simple-language-is\">simple language</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/uncommon-words-and-familiar-words\">uncommon and familiar words</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/fewer-words-cut-prune-redraft\">fewer words</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/the-lecture-s-activity-what-can-go\">what can go</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-pre-cis-8-3-8-4\">The précis (8.3, 8.4)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Précis</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>précis</strong> (said PRAY-see) “says all that has been said in the given passage; but the précis must say that in one-third of all the words in the original passage” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=291s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:51</a>). Two rules: (1) <strong>comprehend</strong> the passage and list its points; (2) write within <strong>one-third</strong> of its words (the number of words ÷ 3). The result is one connected paragraph, not sentences next to bullet points.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Jefferson in 12 words (the lecture’s drafts at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=624s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:24</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Thomas Jefferson is best known for the Declaration of Independence, in which he announced the separation of the 13 British colonies in North America — the right of Americans in these places to govern themselves.</em> (34 words)</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The points: Jefferson declared independence; the 13 British colonies in North America separated; they would govern themselves.</li>\n<li>The limit: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">34</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">11.3</span></span></span></span>,</span> so about 12 words.</li>\n<li>Draft 1 (19 words): <em>Thomas Jefferson announced the separation of the 13 British colonies of North America and their right to govern themselves.</em></li>\n<li>Drafts 2 and 3 (17, then 14 words) turn the sentence round and shrink <em>British colonies of North America</em> to <em>British American colonies</em>.</li>\n<li>Final draft (12 words): <em>The 13 British American colonies would henceforth govern themselves, declared Thomas Jefferson.</em> (<em>Henceforth</em>: from now on.)</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> all three points in 12 words; the numeral <em>13</em> counts as one word. It is rewriting “in the new grammatical pattern”, not “mindless cutting” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The seven techniques (8.4, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=147s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:27</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a. remove redundant words</td>\n<td><em>new and wider meaning</em> → <em>new meaning</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>b. one word for many</td>\n<td><em>In our time</em> → <em>Presently</em>; <em>reaching out with menacing power over vast areas of</em> → <em>threatening</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>c. rewrite sentences</td>\n<td><em>In the present times, they have found a new meaning for …</em> → <em>Jefferson’s sovereignty principle … has seen a new meaning.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>d. indirect speech, in the third person</td>\n<td><em>we have had revealed to us</em> → <em>they have found</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>e. drop illustrations and long lists</td>\n<td><em>in Chicago, in Los Angeles, in New York, or in a nearby studio</em> → <em>here or there</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>f. connect with a few discourse markers</td>\n<td><em>also, besides, but, hence, so, therefore, thus</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>g. numerals for numbers above 9</td>\n<td><em>36</em> (one word), not <em>thirty-six</em> (two)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>With these, a 49-word passage became 32, then 25, then 17 words <span class=\"nwp\">(<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">49</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">16.3</span></span></span></span>,</span> so 17), “and we have not lost anything of the meaning” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=645s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:45</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w08/jefferson-in-12-words-the-first-example\">Jefferson in 12 words</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/a-longer-passage-153-words-into-51\">153 words into 51</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/a-pre-cis-is-a-paragraph\">a précis is a paragraph</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/step-1-read-and-list-the-points\">list the points</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/step-2-seven-techniques\">seven techniques</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/redundant-words-and-one-word-for-many\">one word for many</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/the-drafts-49-words-into-17\">49 words into 17</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/long-lists-of-illustrations\">long lists</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-lab-report-8-5\">The lab report (8.5)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Manual and report</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>manual</strong> comes <strong>before</strong> the work: what to do, and how. A <strong>report</strong> comes <strong>after</strong> it and answers: what was done; why; how (tools, methods, materials, process, other information); time and cost; results; previous work; applications and future work (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=459s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:39</a>). A report is “concrete, it is specific; it describes, it does not discuss”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>“Nearly all sentences are simple sentences, but they are all in passive voice and in the past tense” (the present tense is fine too). The passive leaves out the doer, because anyone who follows the same procedure should get the same result (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Manual (a command)</th>\n<th>Report (passive, past)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Pour a cup of water into it.</em></td>\n<td><em>A cup and a quarter of water was poured into the percolator.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Light the stove.</em></td>\n<td><em>Percolator was switched on and time noted.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Manual = map, report = record</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>m</strong>anual is a <strong>m</strong>ap, drawn before the journey; a <strong>r</strong>eport is a <strong>r</strong>ecord, written after it.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w08/manual-before-report-after\">manual before, report after</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/what-a-report-answers\">what a report answers</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/the-lecture-s-report-making-tea\">the tea report</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w08/the-language-of-a-report\">the language of a report</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 8</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>An SOP that repeats the CV, or gives labels without evidence (<em>hard-working, passionate</em>).</li>\n<li>Clichés and empty phrases: <em>add value to your esteemed organization</em>, <em>complete dedication</em>, <em>prove myself</em> (“to whom and for what?”).</li>\n<li>✗ <em>a SOP</em> → <em>an SOP</em>; ✗ <em>looking forward to have</em> → <em>looking forward to having</em>.</li>\n<li>Boasting (“I am a class by myself”), or hiding what you learnt out of humility.</li>\n<li>A précis that drops a point, cuts words mindlessly, or reads like bullet points.</li>\n<li>A lab report written as commands, or in the first person (<em>I poured …</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w08\">Week 8</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-9-punctuation-and-the-four-language-skills\">Week 9: Punctuation and the four language skills</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In speech, pauses and the voice show where an idea ends; in writing, <strong>punctuation</strong> does that job. Week 9 gives the rules for the comma, the semicolon, the colon and the apostrophe (9.1, 9.2). Then it asks what makes communication effective, and how to develop the four <strong>integrated skills</strong>: listening, speaking, reading and writing (9.3–9.8).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-comma-9-1\">The comma (9.1)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Use of the comma</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>between list items, except before the last (a comma before <em>and</em> is optional; two items take none)</td>\n<td><em>I have to buy cereals, pulses, milk, and eggs from the supermarket.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>after words outside the main sentence: sentence adverbs, names, letter greetings</td>\n<td><em>Unfortunately, I didn’t attend the class.</em> · <em>Riya, can you please hold this bucket for a second?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>around linking words: one after (at the start), two (in the middle), one before (at the end)</td>\n<td><em>However, everyone likes her.</em> · <em>Everyone, however, likes her.</em> · <em>Everyone likes her, however.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>before a quotation</td>\n<td><em>He said, “It’s a warm day.”</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In letters, British English writes <em>Dear Sir,</em> (a comma) and American English <em>Dear Sir:</em> (a colon); the closing (<em>Yours faithfully,</em>) takes a comma in both (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Commas between clauses (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>independent</strong> clause (I) is a complete sentence; a <strong>dependent</strong> clause (D) is not (<em>When Lena gets home from work</em> … “so what?”).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>I + I: a comma <strong>and</strong> a coordinating conjunction (<em>and, or, but, so</em>): <em>He kicked the ball, and they won the match.</em> A comma alone is an error, a <strong>comma splice</strong>.</li>\n<li>D + I: a comma, and no <em>and</em>: <em>If you get good grades, I will take you to London.</em></li>\n<li>I + D: <strong>no</strong> comma: <em>He was so happy when he won the match!</em></li>\n<li>An <strong>essential</strong> relative clause (remove it and the sentence breaks) takes no commas; a <strong>non-essential</strong> one takes commas.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Essential or not? (the lecture’s examples at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>That man is the criminal who shot his neighbour last month.</em> Remove the clause: <em>That man is the criminal.</em> “What criminal?” The clause is essential: <strong>no</strong> comma.</li>\n<li><em>People love to celebrate New Year’s Eve, which is the last day of the year.</em> Without the clause the sentence still “makes perfect sense”: non-essential, so a comma.</li>\n<li><em>My cousin Nisha, whom you met at my home, is heading to US tomorrow.</em> Non-essential and in the middle: <strong>two</strong> commas.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> essential → no commas; non-essential → commas (two in the middle of a sentence).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w09/items-in-a-list\">items in a list</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/words-outside-the-main-sentence\">words outside the main sentence</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/linking-words\">linking words</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/clauses\">clauses</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/essential-and-non-essential-relative-clauses\">essential and non-essential</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/before-a-quotation\">before a quotation</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/practice-commas\">practice</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"semicolon-colon-and-apostrophe-9-2\">Semicolon, colon and apostrophe (9.2)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>semicolon</strong> (;) joins two closely related independent clauses <strong>without</strong> a conjunction: <em>I thought it was terrible; they thought it was great.</em> ✗ <em>I thought it was terrible; but they thought it was great.</em> A <strong>conjunctive adverb</strong> (<em>however, in fact, therefore</em>) stays, with its comma: <em>She has not got many friends; however, everyone likes her.</em> · <em>My dog knows many tricks; in fact, he can bark on command.</em></li>\n<li>The <strong>colon</strong> (:) comes only <strong>after a complete sentence</strong>, and introduces a list or a clause: <em>I have several favorite genres of movies: drama, science fiction, and mystery.</em> · <em>Never forget this point: think before you speak.</em> ✗ <em>Never forget this point that: you must think before you speak.</em></li>\n<li>Leave no space before ; or :, and one space after.</li>\n<li>The <strong>apostrophe</strong> (') stands where letters are left out: <em>doesn’t</em> (✗ <em>does’nt</em>); never contract three words (<em>They’re not</em> or <em>They aren’t</em>, ✗ <em>They’re’nt</em>). It marks <strong>possession</strong>: <em>Liya’s new pet</em>; for a singular noun in <em>-s</em>, <em>Shreyas’</em> or <em>Shreyas’s</em>; for a plural in <em>-s</em>, <em>two years’ experience</em>. <strong>Possessive pronouns</strong> (<em>yours, ours, its</em>) never take one (✗ <em>Your’s sincerely</em>). <em>It’s</em> means <em>it is</em> or <em>it has</em>: <em>It’s time to start the debate</em> / <em>The cat bit its nail</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Joining two independent clauses</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>comma + conjunction</td>\n<td><em>He kicked the ball, and they won the match.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>semicolon, no conjunction</td>\n<td><em>It was a dark night; the moon hid behind a cloud.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>semicolon + conjunctive adverb + comma</td>\n<td><em>My dog knows many tricks; in fact, he can bark on command.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>✗ a comma alone</td>\n<td>✗ <em>He kicked the ball, they won the match.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The <em>it is</em> test</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Read <em>it’s</em> as <em>it is</em> or <em>it has</em>. If the sentence still makes sense, write <em>it’s</em>; if not (<em>The dog bit it is tail</em>), write <em>its</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w09/the-semicolon\">the semicolon</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/semicolons-with-conjunctive-adverbs\">with conjunctive adverbs</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/the-colon\">the colon</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/practice-commas-semicolons-and-colons\">practice</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/the-apostrophe-in-contractions\">contractions</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/the-apostrophe-of-possession\">possession</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/possessive-pronouns-its-and-it-s\">its and it’s</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/practice-apostrophes\">practice</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"effective-communication-9-3\">Effective communication (9.3)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Myths</strong>: a good speaker speaks very fast, uses ornamental long sentences and <strong>low-frequency</strong> words (rare words), or puts on a “fake accent”. What counts is <strong>communicability</strong>: the message the receiver actually gets (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Communication is <strong>you-centric</strong> (centred on the receiver), not I-centric. It happens only when the message is received “without any distortion and ambiguity”.</li>\n<li>Three premises (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>): it is a <strong>two-way</strong> process; it is an <strong>exchange and progression</strong> of ideas, with <strong>feedback</strong> (the receiver’s response); it moves towards a <strong>mutually accepted goal</strong>.</li>\n<li>An effective message is brief, clear, objective, precise, simple, intelligible and useful (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">LSRW: the four integrated skills (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Listening</strong> and <strong>reading</strong> are <strong>receptive</strong> skills (input); <strong>speaking</strong> and <strong>writing</strong> are <strong>productive</strong> skills (output). Receptive does not mean passive: the mind is very active. Listening feeds speaking, and reading feeds writing. A child <strong>acquires</strong> listening and speaking naturally; reading and writing are <strong>learnt</strong>, with instruction (Lecture 9.6).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w09/myths-about-good-speakers\">myths</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/what-communication-is\">what communication is</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/three-premises\">three premises</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/attributes-of-an-effective-communicator\">attributes</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/the-four-integrated-skills\">the four skills</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/what-each-skill-needs\">what each skill needs</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"speaking-9-4\">Speaking (9.4)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The challenges (anxiety, nervousness, lack of confidence, <strong>hypercorrection</strong> from the fear of mistakes, scattered ideas, a monotone voice, a stiff body, distractions) are “interdependent”, and so are the cures: control of the content brings confidence, and fluency follows (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Prepare: know your audience and your topic, organise the information, give a proper introduction, logical arguments, emphasis and a conclusion. A little pause before an important idea draws attention, and a pause also “works wonder when you changed the idea” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Speak <strong>intelligibly</strong>, <strong>fluently</strong> and <strong>confidently</strong>. <strong>Fluency</strong> “refers to the continuity of idea and the content, not the speed of delivery”: the usual speed is 120–150 words a minute, and “anything above 100 words per minute is sufficient” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2460s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 41:00</a>). <strong>Paraphrase</strong> (say it again in other words) when the audience does not follow.</li>\n<li><strong>Appropriacy</strong>: every word has its own shade of meaning (<em>incident, accident, disaster, calamity</em>) and its own partners: <em>strong coffee</em> (✗ <em>powerful coffee</em>), <em>a sharp knife</em> (✗ <em>an intelligent knife</em>).</li>\n<li>Vowel length changes meaning: <em>sin/seen, wet/wait, get/gate</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>). English cannot drop the subject (<em>It is raining</em>, ✗ <em>Is raining</em>), except in imperatives (<em>Come in</em>), where the subject <em>you</em> is obvious.</li>\n<li>Spoken English joins sounds (<em>bad doll</em> with one /d/; <em>fast train</em> said “fas-train”), uses weak and contracted forms, half-finished sentences, <strong>deictic</strong> (pointing) words such as <em>over there</em>, and fillers. Its strategies: greeting, opening, turn-taking and turn-giving, holding the floor, paraphrasing, clarifying, body language, interpreting feedback.</li>\n<li>Keep useful expressions ready: <em>What I would like to talk about today is …</em> · <em>Now I will move on to …</em> · <em>To sum up, …</em> · <em>I agree with what you are saying, but …</em> · <em>What do you think …?</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w09/challenges-in-speaking-in-public\">challenges</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/making-a-presentation-effective\">an effective presentation</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/effective-speaking\">effective speaking</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/linguistic-skills\">linguistic skills</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/interactive-skills\">interactive skills</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/pronunciation-and-vocabulary\">pronunciation and vocabulary</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/structure-and-fluency\">structure and fluency</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/communication-strategies\">strategies</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/characteristics-of-spoken-english\">spoken English</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/useful-expressions\">useful expressions</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/prepare-before-you-appear\">prepare before you appear</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"listening-and-reading-9-5-9-6\">Listening and reading (9.5, 9.6)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Hearing and listening (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Hearing</strong> is physical: sound waves move the eardrums, and impulses travel to the brain. <strong>Listening</strong> is mental: “paying close attention to, and making sense of, what we hear” (Stephen Lucas). Active listening has three steps: <strong>hearing → understanding → judging</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Rost’s listening competences include telling sounds apart, recognising words, noticing stress, using background knowledge to predict, recalling key words, giving feedback and <strong>reformulating</strong> (saying it again in your own way). Kratz and Kratz’s tips: ask questions, take notes, watch the speaker’s body language, keep an open mind, do not interrupt, seek clarity, and listen to the tone. Real speech is “idiosyncratic and fuzzy”: sounds merge, words are dropped, and the speed varies (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Skimming and scanning (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Skimming</strong> is glancing rapidly through a text for its <strong>gist</strong> (main idea), with nothing specific in mind, like skimming the cream off milk. <strong>Scanning</strong> is darting over a text for one specific piece of information, with a purpose decided in advance, like a CT scan of one part of the body. The lecturer scans the morning paper: the sports page, then the cricket news, then the scoreboard (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Reading has three stages: <strong>pre-reading</strong> (anticipate the content), <strong>while-reading</strong> (notes, keywords, “connecting the dots”) and <strong>post-reading</strong> (order what you collected, question it, re-read). <strong>Intensive</strong> reading goes deep into one text (poetry is the lecture’s example); <strong>extensive</strong> reading goes wide, over many texts on a theme. Every word has three sides: its <strong>structure</strong> (spelling and pronunciation), its <strong>meaning</strong> and its <strong>context</strong> (<em>the neck of a bottle</em>). There are “no golden rules”: what works is sustained reading of texts you choose.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w09/hearing-and-listening\">hearing and listening</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/three-steps-hearing-understanding-judging\">three steps</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/rost-s-listening-competences\">Rost</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/kratz-and-kratz-tips-for-listeners\">Kratz and Kratz</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/spoken-english-is-fuzzy\">fuzzy speech</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/what-good-listening-demands\">what listening demands</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/reading-as-a-skill\">reading as a skill</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/pre-reading-while-reading-post-reading\">three stages</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/skimming-and-scanning\">skimming and scanning</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/acquired-and-learnt\">acquired and learnt</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/three-sides-of-every-word\">three sides of a word</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/intensive-and-extensive-reading\">intensive and extensive</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/what-to-read\">what to read</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"writing-coder-and-power-9-7-9-8\">Writing: CODER and POWER (9.7, 9.8)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Writing needs vocabulary, functional grammar, context, organised ideas, <strong>cohesive devices</strong> (connectors), punctuation (“full stop is complete pause”), and <strong>paragraphs</strong> built around one <strong>nuclear sentence</strong>, with the other sentences supporting it.</li>\n<li>Writing is not speech written down: avoid repetition and write complete sentences. Simple beats ornamental. Mind language and socio-cultural appropriacy and <strong>gender neutrality</strong>, and avoid vague, generalised, categorical (sweeping) and derogatory (insulting) remarks.</li>\n<li>Good writing is brief, clear, succinct, simple, specific, chronological (each sentence feeding the next), unambiguous, written for its audience, logical, coherent and lucid. Two ambiguities: <em>Flying planes can be dangerous</em> (planes in the air, or flying them?) and <em>that old man and woman</em> (is she old too?) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>CODER (9.7)</th>\n<th>POWER (9.8)</th>\n<th>The common idea</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Collect</td>\n<td>Prepare (brainstorm, without judging)</td>\n<td>gather all the ideas first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Organize</td>\n<td>Organise (first, next, last; strike off what does not fit)</td>\n<td>a logical order for the reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Draft</td>\n<td>Write (full sentences, a first draft)</td>\n<td>write without polishing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Edit</td>\n<td>Evaluate (vocabulary, spelling, grammar, punctuation)</td>\n<td>correct the gaps and the slips</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Review</td>\n<td>Review (later, aloud, with a second reader)</td>\n<td>read it as the reader will</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In POWER’s example, a letter asking the librarian to refund a caution deposit of 2,000 rupees is evaluated: <em>semenster</em> → <em>semester</em>; <em>2000</em> → <em>Rs.2000</em>; the <em>Student Book Loan</em> scheme gets capitals; <em>Please write back to me</em> → <em>Therefore, I request you to make arrangements for the refund at the earliest</em>; and the letter gets <em>Dear Sir/Madam,</em> and a formal closing (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w09/prerequisites\">prerequisites</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/the-coder-principle\">CODER</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/planning-and-executing-the-act-of-writing\">planning</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/characteristics-of-good-writing\">good writing</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/write-well-the-power-method\">POWER</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w09/coder-and-power-side-by-side\">CODER and POWER</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 9</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A comma splice: ✗ <em>He kicked the ball, they won the match.</em></li>\n<li>✗ <em>If you get good grades, and I will take you to London.</em> ✗ <em>He was so happy, when he won the match!</em></li>\n<li>A semicolon with a conjunction (✗ <em>; but</em>). But keep a conjunctive adverb after it (<em>; in fact,</em>).</li>\n<li>A colon after an incomplete clause (✗ <em>… this point that:</em>).</li>\n<li>✗ <em>does’nt</em>, ✗ <em>They’re’nt</em>, ✗ <em>Your’s</em>, ✗ <em>it’s</em> for <em>its</em>, ✗ <em>two years’s</em>.</li>\n<li>Thinking listening and reading are passive: they are receptive, and active.</li>\n<li>Thinking fluency is speed; rare words and fake accents are myths.</li>\n<li>Looking for “golden rules” of reading or writing: practise.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w09\">Week 9</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-10-passives-participles-negatives-reported-speech-condi\">Week 10: Passives, participles, negatives, reported speech, conditionals</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Five grammar lectures, each built on the template subject + predicate. A <strong>passive</strong> hides the doer. A <strong>participle</strong> is a verb form that describes. A <strong>negative</strong> pulls the tense out of the verb and allows words like <em>anything</em>. <strong>Reported speech</strong> embeds what someone said. A <strong>conditional</strong> ties a result to a condition.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"passives-hiding-the-doer-10-1\">Passives: hiding the doer (10.1)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The function and form of a passive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Function</strong>: to <strong>suppress agency</strong>, that is, to hold back who did the action (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>). Whatever stands in the subject position is the <strong>focus</strong>, so the passive puts there what the action was done to. <strong>Form</strong>: <strong>tense + <em>be</em> + V3</strong> (also written <em>be</em> + <em>en</em> + V3). The <strong>agent</strong> (the doer) can come back at the end as an optional <strong>adjunct</strong>: <em>by the principal</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>The classes were suspended (by the principal)</em> (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The doer is not important, and the classes are the focus, so <em>the classes</em> takes the subject position.</li>\n<li>The event is past and the subject plural: <em>be</em> + past + plural = <em>were</em>.</li>\n<li>Add V3: <em>suspended</em>. As a participle, it takes no object.</li>\n<li>If you wish, add the agent as an adjunct: <em>by the principal</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>The classes were suspended (by the principal).</em> Here the subject, <em>the classes</em>, is not the agent.</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>The lecture rejects the popular rule “the object becomes the subject”: active and passive are <strong>two separate sentences</strong>. In the passive, <em>the classes</em> simply is the subject, and <em>by the principal</em> is an adjunct, not an object (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Only transitive verbs have passives: <em>I slept at nine last night</em> and <em>I went to Delhi last week</em> have none.</li>\n<li>The agency is suppressed in <em>The letters were sent</em>, <em>The culprits were booked</em>, <em>The meeting was adjourned</em>. When the doer <strong>is</strong> the point, keep the active.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w10/what-a-passive-sentence-is-for\">what a passive is for</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/the-subject-is-the-place-of-focus\">the place of focus</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/active-and-passive-side-by-side\">side by side</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/subject-and-agent-are-not-the-same-thing\">subject and agent</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/does-the-object-become-the-subject\">does the object become the subject?</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/how-a-passive-is-built-tense-be-v3\">tense + be + V3</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/only-transitive-verbs-have-a-passive\">transitive verbs only</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/the-purpose-once-more\">the purpose</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/when-not-to-use-a-passive\">when not to use it</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"participles-and-gerunds-10-2\">Participles and gerunds (10.2)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Participle (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A form made from a verb that is <strong>not the main verb</strong> of its sentence and works like an adjective: it <strong>modifies</strong> a noun. <em>Raju was wearing a <strong>torn</strong> shirt.</em> · <em>The house looked <strong>abandoned</strong>.</em> (but in <em>Raju <strong>abandoned</strong> the house</em> it is the main verb) · <em><strong>Running</strong> nose is not a good symptom.</em> There are two forms: V + <em>-ing</em> (<em>breaking news, sleeping tiger</em>) and V + <em>-ed</em> or V3 (<em>baked potatoes, fried vegetables</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>“Present participle” and “past participle” are only <strong>names</strong>: “they have very little to do with either present or past”. The tense sits on the main verb, so participles carry <strong>no tense</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>). A <strong>gerund</strong> is V + <em>-ing</em> used as a <strong>noun</strong>: <em><strong>Swimming</strong> is fun.</em> · <em><strong>Running</strong> reduces risk of heart-attack.</em> Then another word must be the verb (<em>is, reduces</em>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Three questions for any -ing or -ed word</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Is it the main verb, carrying the tense? <em>I <strong>was looking</strong> for an old book</em> → the main verb.</li>\n<li>Does it describe a noun? <em>a <strong>torn</strong> shirt</em> → a participle.</li>\n<li>Is it itself the subject or the object? <em><strong>Swimming</strong> is fun</em> → a gerund.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w10/six-sentences-to-start\">six sentences</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/two-types-ing-and-ed\">-ing and -ed</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/participles-work-like-adjectives\">participles as adjectives</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/gerunds-ing-forms-used-as-nouns\">gerunds</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"negation-and-negative-sensitive-words-10-3\">Negation and negative-sensitive words (10.3)</h3>\n<p>Learning a language joins <strong>grammatical competence</strong> (the rules of structure, inside the mind: the <strong>I-language</strong>) and <strong>pragmatic competence</strong> (the rules of use: the <strong>E-language</strong>), at the same time. A child’s mind, with its <strong>Language Acquisition Device</strong> (LAD) and <strong>Universal Grammar</strong> (UG), turns fuzzy, limited input into grammatical, infinite output, without effort; an adult must pay attention (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Negation and negative-sensitive elements</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>English has two negative words, <em>no</em> and <em>not</em>. In a negative sentence the tense comes out of the verb: <strong>tense + negation + verb</strong>: <em>John likes novels</em> → <em>John <strong>does not like</strong> novels</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>). A <strong>negative-sensitive element</strong> occurs only when the sentence is negative: <em>anybody, anyone, anything, any, a word, a penny, a red cent, at all</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Good</th>\n<th>Not good</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>John did not like anything.</em></td>\n<td>✗ <em>John liked anything.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Mary did not meet with anyone.</em></td>\n<td>✗ <em>Mary met with anyone.</em> (say <em>someone</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Raju did not speak a word.</em> (= nothing at all)</td>\n<td><em>Raju spoke a word.</em> (literally one word only)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Sarita did not speak at all.</em></td>\n<td>✗ <em>Sarita spoke at all.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Did you meet with anyone?</em></td>\n<td>✗ <em>Did you get a penny?</em> (meaning “anything at all”)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>Somebody, someone, something</em> are not negative-sensitive. The <em>any</em>-words can also appear in <strong>questions</strong>; <em>a word</em> and <em>a penny</em> need negation only (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w10/learning-a-language-two-kinds-of-knowledge\">two kinds of knowledge</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/learning-language-is-child-s-play\">child’s play</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/negation-the-tense-comes-out-of-the-verb\">the tense comes out</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/words-that-need-a-negative\">words that need a negative</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/a-word-a-penny-a-red-cent-at-all\">a word, a penny</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/questions-the-one-other-place\">questions</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reported-speech-10-4\">Reported speech (10.4)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Reported speech (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“A way of reporting someone’s utterances without quoting their exact words”, also called <strong>indirect speech</strong>. It has a <strong>reporting clause</strong> (independent: <em>Raman said</em>; verbs like <em>say, tell, ask, reply, complain, suggest</em>, usually in the simple past) and a <strong>reported clause</strong> (embedded: a <em>that</em>-clause, an <em>if</em>/<em>whether</em> clause, a <em>to</em>-infinitive or an <em>-ing</em> clause). Reporting also lets the reporter <strong>soften</strong> what was said.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Raman said, “I will join you for the meeting.”</em> (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Keep the reporting clause: <em>Raman said</em>.</li>\n<li>Drop the inverted commas, and join with <em>that</em>.</li>\n<li>Change the pronouns to the reporter’s point of view: <em>I</em> → <em>he</em>; <em>you</em> → <em>us</em> (or <em>them</em>).</li>\n<li>Move the verb one step back: <em>will</em> → <em>would</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Raman said that he would join us for the meeting.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Direct speech</th>\n<th>Reported clause</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>is</em> (simple present)</td>\n<td><em>was</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>is leaving</em> (present continuous)</td>\n<td><em>was leaving</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>have won</em> (present perfect)</td>\n<td><em>had won</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>have been writing</em> (present perfect continuous)</td>\n<td><em>had been writing</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>left</em> (simple past)</td>\n<td><em>had left</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>will</em></td>\n<td><em>would</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>This shift is <strong>backshifting</strong>. Time words move too: <em>tonight</em> becomes <em>that night</em> (and say <em>tonight, this morning</em>, never <em>today night</em>). <em>Say</em> reports questions, greetings and wishes; <em>tell</em> takes an object pronoun: <em>Ravi told <strong>me</strong> that Radhika was leaving for England that night</em>. There is no backshift when the reporting verb is present (<em>He says that she is a smart person</em>), for facts and habits (<em>She said that the sun sets in the west</em>), or when the content is still true (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1296s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:36</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w10/why-we-report\">why we report</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/the-reporting-clause\">the reporting clause</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/say-or-tell\">say or tell</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/the-reported-clause\">the reported clause</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/changing-the-pronouns\">pronouns</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/changing-the-tense-backshifting\">backshifting</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/when-the-tense-stays\">when the tense stays</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"conditionals-10-5\">Conditionals (10.5)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Conditional sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A complex sentence: an <em>if</em>-clause (the <strong>condition</strong>, a subordinate clause) + an independent clause (the <strong>result</strong>). The <em>if</em>-clause may come first or second; <strong>when it comes first, put a comma after it</strong>: <em>If Naina rings me, I will invite her for lunch at home</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>zero: facts, rules</td>\n<td>present + present</td>\n<td><em>The clay becomes sticky if you add water to it.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>first: a real possibility</td>\n<td>present + <em>will</em></td>\n<td><em>If the price of orange rises, demand will fall.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>second: unreal, unlikely</td>\n<td>past + <em>could/would/might</em></td>\n<td><em>If we had an oven, we could grill the mushrooms.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>second, with <em>be</em></td>\n<td><em>were</em> for every subject</td>\n<td><em>If I were you, I would fix the leakage on my own.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>third: an imagined past</td>\n<td>past perfect + <em>would have</em> + V3</td>\n<td><em>If I had prepared well, I would have scored good grades.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mixed: a past change, a present result</td>\n<td>past perfect + <em>would</em></td>\n<td><em>If I had not won the lottery, I would not be on a world tour.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mixed: a present change, a different past</td>\n<td>past + <em>would have</em> + V3</td>\n<td><em>If it wasn’t urgent, I wouldn’t have called you today.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The last one is also <strong>polite</strong>: it tells the listener that you would not disturb them without a reason (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>). In the second conditional the past form marks an unreal situation, not past time: <em>If we had an oven</em> means we do not have one now.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w10/order-and-the-comma\">order and the comma</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/zero-conditional-facts\">zero</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/first-conditional-real-possibilities\">first</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/second-conditional-unreal-situations\">second</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/if-i-were-you\">If I were you</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/third-conditional-an-imagined-past\">third</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/mixed-conditionals\">mixed</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/conditionals-for-politeness\">politeness</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w10/all-the-types-together\">all the types</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 10</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>“In a passive the object becomes the subject”: the lecture rejects this. <em>By the principal</em> is an adjunct, not an object.</li>\n<li>A passive of an intransitive verb (none for <em>I slept at nine last night</em>).</li>\n<li>Treating “present” and “past” participle as times: participles carry no tense.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that a gerund is a noun: the sentence still needs a verb.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>John liked anything</em>; ✗ <em>Mary met with anyone</em>; ✗ <em>Sarita spoke at all</em>.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>today night</em> → <em>tonight</em>; in a report, <em>that night</em>.</li>\n<li>Backshifting a fact, or after a present reporting verb: ✓ <em>She said that the sun sets in the west.</em></li>\n<li>A comma before an <em>if</em>-clause that comes second: ✓ <em>I might apply for a loan if I had to build a house.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w10\">Week 10</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-11-quantifiers-contrast-words-vocabulary-and-connecting\">Week 11: Quantifiers, contrast words, vocabulary and connecting ideas</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Week 11 is about small words with large effects, and about where words come from. <strong>Quantifiers</strong> say how much or how many. <strong>Contrast words</strong> and <strong>connectors</strong> join ideas within and between sentences. <strong>Morphemes</strong> and the processes of word formation show how English builds words, and how it borrows them from about 120 languages.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"quantifiers-11-1-11-2\">Quantifiers (11.1, 11.2)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Quantifier</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>quantifier</strong> is a <strong>determiner</strong> (a word before a noun, like <em>a, the, my, some</em>) that tells <strong>how many</strong>, with <strong>countable</strong> nouns (<em>children, guests</em>), or <strong>how much</strong>, with <strong>uncountable</strong> nouns (<em>water, money, time</em>). Before a noun it works like an adjective; alone it works like a pronoun (<em>No, there aren’t <strong>any</strong></em>); and it can combine with other determiners (<em><strong>All the four</strong> eggs were bad</em>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=z-ILfHZhyc8&amp;t=133s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:13</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>With countable nouns</th>\n<th>With uncountable nouns</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a large number or amount</td>\n<td><em>many</em>: <em>Many workers were trapped inside the mines.</em></td>\n<td><em>much</em>: <em>Is there much work left?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>large, for both kinds</td>\n<td><em>a lot of, lots of</em>: <em>The dancers wore a lot of bangles.</em></td>\n<td><em>a lot of, lots of</em>: <em>They have got a lot of money.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>small, positive (“some”)</td>\n<td><em>a few</em>: <em>A few teachers will accompany the students on the trip.</em></td>\n<td><em>a little</em>: <em>Rub a little moisturizer on the face for hydration.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>small, negative (“hardly any”)</td>\n<td><em>few</em>: <em>Few apples from the shop were good.</em></td>\n<td><em>little</em>: <em>Little knowledge is dangerous.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>enough, or more than enough</td>\n<td><em>plenty of</em>: <em>Anita has plenty of toys.</em></td>\n<td><em>enough, loads of</em>: <em>There is loads of time left for my trip to Shimla.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a non-specific amount</td>\n<td><em>some</em>, <em>any</em>: <em>There weren’t any cans left in the tray.</em></td>\n<td><em>some</em>, <em>any</em>: <em>Is there any milk left?</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Some</em> goes in affirmative sentences, <em>any</em> in negatives and questions: ✗ <em>I have any money</em>.</li>\n<li>More with <em>many</em> and <em>much</em>: <em>not many</em> (= few: <em>Not many people are concerned about climate change</em>); <em>as many as two thousand people</em> (a surprisingly high number); <em>ten medals in as many years</em> (in ten years); <em>a great many</em>; <em>too many</em>, <em>too much</em> (more than acceptable); <em>much of his life</em>; <em>as much information as you can</em>; <em>much better</em>.</li>\n<li><em>A bit</em> softens: <em>I was a bit bored at the concert</em>; <em>It was a little bit difficult to agree with his demands</em>.</li>\n<li><em><strong>All (of)</strong></em> = everyone, or the complete amount (<em>All of you must be present at the meet</em>); <em><strong>no</strong></em> = a zero quantity (<em>No work is allotted for you today</em>); <em><strong>more</strong></em> = an additional quantity (<em>More and more people are applying for this job position</em>); <em><strong>most</strong></em> = the majority (<em>Most work</em> in general, <em>Most of the work</em> in particular); <em><strong>less</strong></em> = a reduced amount (<em>This refrigerator consumes less power</em>; after a verb: <em>The tickets cost less</em>); <em><strong>several</strong></em> = more than one but not many.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Singular or plural verb?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>both</em> (two, together) → plural: <em>Both of them are from the same department.</em></li>\n<li><em>either</em> (one of two) and <em>neither</em> (not one of two) → singular: <em>Neither ring fits me.</em> <em>Neither of them was fit for the job.</em></li>\n<li><em>each</em> and <em>every</em> (members one by one) → singular: <em>Every child deserves good education.</em> <em>Every</em> also marks intervals: <em>every ten minutes</em>, <em>every December</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w11/countable-and-uncountable-nouns\">countable and uncountable</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/three-more-facts-about-quantifiers\">three facts</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/many-with-countable-nouns\">many</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/more-constructions-with-many\">more with many</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/much-with-uncountable-nouns\">much</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/more-constructions-with-much\">more with much</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/a-lot-of-and-lots-of-with-both-kinds-of-noun\">a lot of</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/small-quantities-a-few-few-a-little-little-a-bit\">small quantities</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/a-bit\">a bit</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/enough-plenty-and-more-sufficient-amounts\">enough, plenty</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/some-and-any-non-specific-amounts\">some and any</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/all-and-no\">all and no</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/more\">more</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/most\">most</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/less\">less</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/both-either-and-neither-two-people-or-things\">both, either, neither</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/each-and-every\">each and every</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"contrast-words-11-3\">Contrast words (11.3)</h3>\n<p>Every contrast word joins a <strong>result</strong> to an <strong>expectation</strong> that it opposes. The lecture’s two ideas: <em>I invited Vaiga to the party. She didn’t come.</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pLPzXo8xgBA&amp;t=73s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:13</a>)</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>but</em></td>\n<td>the simplest</td>\n<td><em>I invited Vaiga to the party but she didn’t come.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>although</em></td>\n<td>formal; a clause follows</td>\n<td><em>Vaiga didn’t come to the party although I invited her.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>though</em></td>\n<td>informal, mostly in speech; a clause follows</td>\n<td><em>Though I invited Vaiga to the party, she didn’t come.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>even though</em></td>\n<td>stronger: it shows disappointment; a clause follows</td>\n<td><em>Vaiga didn’t come to the party even though I invited her.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>however</em></td>\n<td>the formal <em>but</em>; no contractions</td>\n<td><em>I invited Vaiga to the party; however, she did not come.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>despite</em>, <em>in spite of</em></td>\n<td>the same meaning; a <strong>phrase</strong> follows</td>\n<td><em>Reva decided to buy an expensive car despite her mother’s objections.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>What follows <em>although</em> or <em>even though</em> gets the emphasis (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pLPzXo8xgBA&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). A <strong>clause</strong> has a subject and a verb (<em>her mother objected</em>); a <strong>phrase</strong> does not (<em>her mother’s objections</em>). And never <em>despite of</em>: it “is absolutely wrong” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pLPzXo8xgBA&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w11/but\">but</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/although-and-though\">although and though</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/even-though\">even though</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/however\">however</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/despite-and-in-spite-of\">despite and in spite of</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"words-and-morphemes-11-4\">Words and morphemes (11.4)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Morpheme (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KGGeRd7Y-98&amp;t=258s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:18</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The smallest units of language that have a meaning or a grammatical function”. <strong>Free</strong> morphemes stand alone as words; <strong>bound</strong> morphemes must be attached to another morpheme.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Free <strong>lexical</strong> morphemes (nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs: <em>dog, run, happy, quickly</em>) form an <strong>open class</strong>: new ones keep coming.</li>\n<li>Free <strong>functional</strong> morphemes (prepositions, conjunctions, articles, demonstratives, auxiliaries, pronouns: <em>with, but, the, this, can, me</em>) form a <strong>closed class</strong>: English has hardly added any in the last hundred years.</li>\n<li>Bound morphemes are <strong>bound roots</strong> (<em>-ceive</em> in <em>receive</em>, <em>-tain</em> in <em>contain</em>) and <strong>affixes</strong>: <strong>prefixes</strong> at the start (<em>un-noticed</em>) and <strong>suffixes</strong> at the end (<em>care-less</em>). English has no <strong>infixes</strong> (affixes inside a word).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Nationalization</em>, part by part (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KGGeRd7Y-98&amp;t=258s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:18</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>nationalization</em> (a noun) without <em>-ation</em> is <em>nationalize</em> (a verb).</li>\n<li>Without <em>-ize</em>: <em>national</em> (an adjective).</li>\n<li>Without <em>-al</em>: <em>nation</em> (a noun).</li>\n<li>Split <em>na</em> + <em>tion</em>: neither part means anything, so stop.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>nation</em> + <em>-al</em> + <em>-ize</em> + <em>-ation</em>: four morphemes, and each suffix changes the word class.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Derivation and inflection</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>derivational</strong> affix makes a <strong>new word</strong>, changing the meaning, the word class, or both: <em>un-</em> + <em>happy</em> = <em>unhappy</em>; <em>kind</em> + <em>-ness</em> = <em>kindness</em>. An <strong>inflectional</strong> affix only makes a grammatical form of the <strong>same</strong> word, and the class does not change. English has just <strong>eight</strong> inflections (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KGGeRd7Y-98&amp;t=1837s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:37</a>): <em>-s</em> for the third person singular (<em>speaks</em>), plural <em>-s</em> (<em>books</em>), possessive <em>'s</em> (<em>John’s</em>), past <em>-ed</em> (<em>helped</em>), past participle <em>-en</em> (<em>eaten</em>), <em>-ing</em> (<em>studying</em>), comparative <em>-er</em> (<em>faster</em>) and superlative <em>-est</em> (<em>fastest</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>So <em>-er</em> in <em>worker</em> is derivational (the verb <em>work</em> becomes a noun), but <em>-er</em> in <em>taller</em> is inflectional (still an adjective). Old words also take new meanings (<strong>semantic extension</strong>): a computer <em>mouse</em>, a software <em>window</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w11/words-keep-changing\">words keep changing</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/breaking-a-word-into-parts\">breaking a word into parts</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/the-classification-of-morphemes\">the classification</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/free-morphemes\">free morphemes</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/bound-morphemes\">bound morphemes</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/affixes-by-position-prefix-infix-suffix\">prefix, infix, suffix</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/affixes-by-function-derivational-and-inflectional\">derivational and inflectional</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/derivational-affixes-by-the-word-they-make\">derivational affixes</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"word-formation-processes-11-5\">Word-formation processes (11.5)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Process</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n<th>Lecture examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>clipping</td>\n<td>a word is cut short (at the back, the front, both ends, or in a compound)</td>\n<td><em>exam</em>, <em>phone</em>, <em>flu</em>, <em>advertorial</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>acronymy</td>\n<td>first letters become a word</td>\n<td><em>NATO</em>, <em>laser</em>, <em>scuba</em>, <em>Noida</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>blending</td>\n<td>parts of two words join</td>\n<td><em>brunch</em> (breakfast + lunch), <em>cyborg</em>, <em>guesstimate</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>back-formation</td>\n<td>a real or supposed affix is removed</td>\n<td><em>resurrection</em> → <em>resurrect</em>; <em>postpone</em> → <em>prepone</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>borrowing</td>\n<td>a word is taken from another language</td>\n<td><em>loot, dharna, jungle</em> (Hindi); <em>robot</em> (Czech); <em>yoghurt</em> (Turkish)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>coinage</td>\n<td>a new word, often from a product name</td>\n<td><em>xerox</em>, <em>Kodak</em>, <em>Kleenex</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>compounding</td>\n<td>two words make one</td>\n<td><em>lipstick, fast food, breakfast, waterproof</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>inflection</td>\n<td>a grammatical form of the same word</td>\n<td><em>go, goes, going, went, gone</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>derivation</td>\n<td>an affix makes a new word</td>\n<td><em>kind</em> → <em>kindness</em>; <em>modern</em> → <em>modernize</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A compound has its own meaning: <em>mustard oil</em> is oil made from mustard, but <em>baby oil</em> is oil for babies (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oLZoS0mwhW8&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w11/clipping\">clipping</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/acronymy\">acronymy</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/blending\">blending</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/back-formation\">back-formation</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/borrowing\">borrowing</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/coinage\">coinage</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/compounding\">compounding</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/inflection-and-derivation\">inflection and derivation</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"connecting-ideas-11-6\">Connecting ideas (11.6)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Connecting ideas within and between sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZXWMfaRd-AI&amp;t=318s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:18</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Adverbs and prepositional phrases connect ideas <strong>between</strong> sentences: <em>There was no AC in the building. <strong>As a result</strong>, the workers had to be sent home.</em> · <em>We could go for a movie this Diwali. <strong>Alternatively</strong>, we could just stay at home and enjoy the sweets.</em> <strong>Conjunctions</strong> connect ideas <strong>within</strong> one sentence: <em><strong>While</strong> I was waiting, I had a cup of coffee.</em> · <em>I will be wearing a sea-blue shawl <strong>so that</strong> you can see me easily.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Between sentences</th>\n<th>Within a sentence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>contrast, concession</td>\n<td>however, nevertheless, on the other hand, even so</td>\n<td>although, though, even though, while, whereas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cause, result</td>\n<td>therefore, consequently, hence, thus, as a result</td>\n<td>because, since, as, so that, in order to</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>adding</td>\n<td>in addition, furthermore, moreover, likewise, also</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>at the same time</td>\n<td>meanwhile, at the same time</td>\n<td>while, as, when</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>before or after</td>\n<td>then, afterwards, subsequently, before that</td>\n<td>after, before, as soon as</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A <strong>concession</strong> admits a fact that seems to work against the main point. To join two clauses in one sentence, use <strong>semicolon + <em>however</em> + comma</strong>: <em>You could fly via Singapore; however, this isn’t the only way</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZXWMfaRd-AI&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>). <em>Even though</em> (a conjunction) makes one sentence; <em>even so</em> (a prepositional phrase) starts a new one: <em>Much of the power of trade unions has been lost. Even so, their political influence should not be underestimated.</em> And <em>however</em> + an adjective means “no matter how”: <em>however necessary you think it is</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w11/between-sentences-adverbs-and-prepositional-phrases\">between sentences</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/within-a-sentence-conjunctions\">within a sentence</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/connecting-two-clauses\">two clauses</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/connectors-by-function\">connectors by function</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/even-though-even-so-however\">even though, even so, however</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"vocabulary-word-parts-and-loanwords-11-7-11-8\">Vocabulary: word parts and loanwords (11.7, 11.8)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Greek and Latin parts</strong> build low-frequency, “high-cognitive” words (words that need more thought): <em>aer-</em> (air), <em>geo-</em> (earth), <em>hydro-</em> (water), <em>hyper-</em> (more), <em>hypo-</em> (less), <em>inter-</em> (between), <em>intra-</em> (within), <em>ultra-</em> (in excess); <em>-cide</em> (killer), <em>-gen</em> (produce), <em>-logy</em> (the study of), <em>-nomy</em> (knowledge of), <em>-vore</em> (feeding). So a <em>herbivore</em> (<em>herb-</em> + <em>-vore</em>) is a plant-eater.</li>\n<li>English has words from about <strong>120 languages</strong>, with Latin the major source: Celtic (<em>basket, clan</em>), Scandinavian (<em>egg, sky, window</em>), French (<em>government, grammar</em>), Hindi and other Indian languages (<em>karma, jungle, thug, pyjamas, avatar</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Instilingo</strong> (<em>insti</em> + <em>lingo</em>) is the code of IIT Madras students: <em>chumma</em> (Tamil, “simply”), <em>funda</em> (from <em>fundamental</em>), <em>gyan</em> (unnecessary talk). New media brings <em>tweet, unfriend, like, DP, viral</em> and acronyms such as <em>ASAP, LOL, RIP</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Loanwords</strong> as advanced vocabulary. Latin: <em>bona fide</em> (authentic), <em>de facto</em> (existing, but not officially accepted), <em>impromptu</em> (without preparation), <em>quid pro quo</em> (something for something), <em>ad hoc</em> (for one specific case), <em>pro rata</em> (in proportion), <em>modus operandi</em> (a way of doing something), <em>status quo</em> (the current situation). French: <em>à la carte</em>, <em>faux pas</em> (a social mistake; not “faux pax”), <em>en masse</em>, <em>chauffeur</em>, <em>déjà vu</em>, <em>bouquet</em>, <em>souvenir</em>. Italian: <em>vendetta</em>, <em>inferno</em>. Japanese: <em>tsunami</em>, <em>karaoke</em>, <em>emoji</em>, <em>ninja</em> (highly skilled).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Pro rata</em> pay (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pBpCNFpgzXQ&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The agreement: 6 hours a day for 600 rupees.</li>\n<li>Pay for one hour: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">600</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span></span></span></span></span> rupees.</li>\n<li>You work only 4 hours: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">400</span></span></span></span></span> rupees.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 400 rupees. <em>Pro rata</em> pay follows the hours worked.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w11/prefixes-and-suffixes-from-greek-and-latin\">Greek and Latin parts</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/words-from-foreign-languages\">foreign languages</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/instilingo-the-code-of-iit-madras\">Instilingo</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/new-media\">new media</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/words-from-latin\">Latin</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/words-from-french\">French</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/words-from-italian\">Italian</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w11/words-from-japanese\">Japanese</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 11</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>✗ <em>many time</em> (<em>time</em> is uncountable); but <em>many times</em> (occasions) is right.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>a little teachers</em> → <em>a few teachers</em>. And <em>few</em>, <em>little</em> are negative, while <em>a few</em>, <em>a little</em> are positive.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>I have any money</em> → <em>some</em>; ✗ <em>Most of work</em> → <em>Most work</em> or <em>Most of the work</em>; ✗ <em>all you</em> → <em>all of you</em>.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>Neither of the pens were working</em> → <em>was</em>. <em>Either</em> and <em>neither</em> are for two; for three or more, use <em>any</em> and <em>none</em>.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>despite of</em>; ✗ <em>despite her mother objected</em> (a clause) → <em>despite her mother’s objections</em>, or <em>even though her mother objected</em>.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>Much of the power has been lost, even so their influence remains</em> (two sentences joined by a comma).</li>\n<li>Treating every <em>-er</em> alike: <em>worker</em> (derivational) is not <em>taller</em> (inflectional).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w11\">Week 11</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"week-12-pragmatic-competence-context-writing-at-work-and-int\">Week 12: Pragmatic competence, context, writing at work and interviews</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The last week ties the course together. Grammar gives <strong>accuracy</strong>, but communication also needs the <strong>context</strong>: what a sentence means in its situation, the barriers that can distort a message, and Hymes’s SPEAKING model. The rest of the week is writing and speaking for work: essays and research reports, e-mails, the résumé, and job interviews.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"pragmatic-competence-12-1\">Pragmatic competence (12.1)</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Four kinds of competence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Linguistic</strong> (grammatical) competence: knowing the structure of a language. It gives accuracy.</li>\n<li><strong>Pragmatic</strong> competence: knowing what sentences mean when they are used in a real social and cultural context.</li>\n<li><strong>Communicative</strong> competence: being able to use the language to communicate with others.</li>\n<li><strong>Communicative confidence</strong> (the lecturer’s own term): what the three “together build” in a learner of a <strong>target language</strong> (the new language being learnt).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I-language</strong> (internal: competence, the language inside the mind) and <strong>E-language</strong> (external: performance, the language in use) cannot be kept apart while we learn: grammar and use come together (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Learning a language is not one kind of learning among many: it is “the foundation of learning itself” (M.A.K. Halliday, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Speaking is <strong>instinctive</strong>, writing is not: “Man has instinctive tendency to speak … while no child has an instinctive tendency to bake, brew, and write” (Charles Darwin, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=855s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:15</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Chomsky</strong> asked what knowledge of language is, how it is acquired, and how it is put into practice, and he rejected the view that “language learning is a matter of habit formation”. Imperfect, fuzzy input yields perfect learning (the <strong>poverty of stimulus</strong>). <strong>Knowledge of language</strong> “consists of underlying rules that we know but we do not know that we know them”. Children find these rules subconsciously; adults must uncover them consciously (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1605s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:45</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Context is built into a language. The lecture’s Hindi examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Hindi</th>\n<th>In English</th>\n<th>The point</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>raajuu ke do bacce haiN</em> / <em>raajuu ke paas do gaariyaaN haiN</em></td>\n<td>Raju has two kids / Raju has two cars</td>\n<td>Hindi changes the structure for kids and for cars; English uses <em>has</em> for both</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>raajuu ko kalam caahiye</em></td>\n<td>Raju needs (wants) a pen</td>\n<td>Hindi turns a wish for a thing into a need; <em>Raju wants a pen</em> is normal English</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>ghar aao</em> / <em>ghar aaiye</em></td>\n<td>Come home / Please come home</td>\n<td>informal against formal, not an order against a request</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>maiN-ne kab kahaa?</em></td>\n<td>When did I say?</td>\n<td>it means “I did not say so”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“I have to go shopping” (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoHS2K9jmzk&amp;t=3120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 52:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>A friend says: “Let us go watch a movie today.”</li>\n<li>You reply: “I have to go shopping.”</li>\n<li>In words, the reply gives information about shopping, which the friend may not care about.</li>\n<li>From the context, the friend understands one thing very clearly: <strong>no</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> a polite refusal. A plain <em>no</em> “sounds confrontational”, so we give other information and let the hearer read the meaning. “This is applicable in English too.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w12/four-kinds-of-competence\">four kinds of competence</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/i-language-and-e-language\">I-language and E-language</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/learning-a-language-is-learning-to-learn\">learning to learn</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/speaking-is-instinctive-writing-is-learnt\">speaking and writing</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/chomsky-from-habits-to-a-generative-mind\">Chomsky</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/context-is-built-into-a-language\">context is built in</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/request-or-order-the-context-decides\">request or order</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/saying-no-without-saying-no\">saying no without saying no</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"essays-and-research-reports-12-2\">Essays and research reports (12.2)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Listening comes before speaking, and speaking needs soft skills too: knowing “what to speak, what not to speak, more importantly, when to speak and when not to speak” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Writing is learnt with effort, and the reader is alone with the page: “we are not there to speak those things” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). Write every day (in 90 days your writing will change), think before you write, and read carefully.</li>\n<li>Steps to write well: give your writing a structure; be clear on the concepts; <strong>outline</strong> a complex message (pack it like a travel bag, with what the reader needs first on top); anticipate the reader’s questions; do not over-explain; go easy on prepositional phrases; drop the fillers (<em>ah, umm</em>); practise.</li>\n<li>Know your type of essay (argumentative, admissions, persuasive, compare and contrast, personal, expository) and your reader. Re-read and edit before you send: “once you have sent it, there is nothing to undo”.</li>\n<li>A <strong>research report</strong> goes from the research problem to the method, then the body (evidence and argument), then the conclusion. It includes a <strong>literature review</strong> (what others have already written) and a clear <strong>research question</strong>. Research is “a systematic process”: <strong>observation → description → explanation</strong>, in that order (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>).</li>\n<li>A <strong>thesis statement</strong> summarises your main point and previews the support. Support it with <strong>logos</strong> (sound reasoning), <strong>pathos</strong> (a balanced emotional appeal) and <strong>ethos</strong> (your credibility), and use the <strong>Rule of Three</strong>: a strong point, then a stronger one, then your strongest.</li>\n<li>“Impressive does not mean selecting too many adjectives or too many adverbs”; avoid writing too many passive sentences too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rTdNZ0qYvwM&amp;t=2070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w12/listening-speaking-and-the-soft-skills\">listening, speaking, soft skills</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/what-writing-is\">what writing is</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/make-writing-a-habit\">make writing a habit</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/steps-to-write-well\">steps to write well</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/know-your-type-and-your-reader\">your type and your reader</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/writing-a-research-report\">a research report</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/impressive-is-not-ornamental\">impressive is not ornamental</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"decent-e-mails-12-3\">Decent e-mails (12.3)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>The rule</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>From</td>\n<td>your <strong>name</strong>, not just your address</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Subject</td>\n<td>never empty: “as important as the body of the text” (<em>Reg. Sick Leave</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Salutation</td>\n<td><em>Dear Karen</em> or <em>Dear Dr Jones</em>: a title goes with the <strong>last</strong> name (✗ <em>Dear Dr Karen</em>); then a colon (American) or a comma (British)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Body</td>\n<td>get to the point, and be succinct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Closing, signature</td>\n<td>a closing line, <em>Best regards</em> (only the first word capitalised), your name; then your position, workplace and contact</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A succinct body (the lecture’s model e-mail at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Dear Karen: Have you completed a revision of the Marks report, and if so, may I have a copy of it? I would like to take it to my meeting with Rachel tomorrow.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The question comes at once, with no small talk.</li>\n<li>The request: <em>may I have a copy of it?</em></li>\n<li>The reason: the meeting with Rachel tomorrow.</li>\n<li>Nothing else is added.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> a request and its reason, with “no additional information”: a succinct message.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Keep ready phrases for each job: <em>This is to inform you that …</em> (formal) · <em>As we discussed …</em> · <em>Could you confirm if …?</em> · <em>Thanks for getting back to me.</em> · <em>I will keep you posted.</em> Leave an empty line between the parts. Before you press Send: do not reply at once, or in anger; read the mail at least three times (“reading is for checking your content, proofreading is checking your grammaticality”); say why you forward a mail; and write to people one by one rather than to a mass list. Say thank you in words: in English “we must be explicit about these things” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HwlmwClEmao&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w12/the-parts-of-an-e-mail\">the parts of an e-mail</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/subject-lines-and-salutations\">subject lines and salutations</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/useful-phrases\">useful phrases</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/spacing\">spacing</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/rules-before-you-press-send\">before you press Send</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"barriers-and-the-speaking-model-12-4-12-5\">Barriers and the SPEAKING model (12.4, 12.5)</h3>\n<p>“The greatest illusion about communication is that it has taken place” (George Bernard Shaw). The sender <strong>encodes</strong> a message, the receiver <strong>decodes</strong> it and sends <strong>feedback</strong>, and much can go wrong in between (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Barrier</th>\n<th>Examples from the slides</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>physical and mechanical</td>\n<td>noise, distance, timing, information overload</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>language or semantic</td>\n<td>bad translation, jargon, metaphors, unclear connotation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>socio-psychological</td>\n<td>stereotypes, bias, group norms, ethnocentrism (judging others by your own culture)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>organizational</td>\n<td>status, hierarchy, one-way flow, rigid rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>personal</td>\n<td>inattention, premature evaluation, selective omission</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>Time is money</em> against the Hindi <em>Samay balwan hai</em> (“time is all powerful”) is a gap in beliefs, not in words; <em>Why don’t you sit?</em> is a question in form but an <strong>offer</strong> in use (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCOp_u_2j6Q&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>). Good communication is <strong>you-oriented</strong> and two-way: turn-taking and turn-giving, mutual trust, clarity, timing, feedback, empathic listening, flexibility and socio-cultural appropriateness. Keep in mind <strong>who</strong> communicates <strong>what</strong> to <strong>whom</strong>, <strong>when</strong>, <strong>why</strong> and <strong>how</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Communicative competence and the SPEAKING model (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK6jVE62F0M&amp;t=430s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:10</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Dell Hymes coined <strong>communicative competence</strong> (1966) because Chomsky’s linguistic competence leaves out use: knowing a language means knowing its <strong>forms</strong> and its <strong>functions</strong>. He grouped the parts of a speech event under the letters of <strong>SPEAKING</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>etting and scene: the time and place, and how formal or serious it is;</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>articipants: the speaker, the addressee, other hearers;</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>nds: the purposes, goals and outcomes;</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>ct sequence: the order of speech acts, turn-taking, interrupting;</li>\n<li><strong>K</strong>ey: the tone, manner or spirit;</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nstrumentalities: the channel, the language, the dialect or the <strong>register</strong> (the language of one setting);</li>\n<li><strong>N</strong>orms: the social rules (when to speak, how loud, which topics);</li>\n<li><strong>G</strong>enre: the kind of event (gossip, a joke, a lecture, a debate).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Every word is learnt with three things: its spelling and pronunciation, its meaning, <strong>and</strong> the context of its use. Each person also has an <strong>idiolect</strong>, an individual style of language.</p>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w12/the-communication-cycle\">the communication cycle</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/1-physical-and-mechanical-barriers\">physical</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/2-language-or-semantic-barriers\">semantic</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/3-socio-psychological-barriers\">socio-psychological</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/4-organizational-barriers\">organizational</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/5-personal-barriers\">personal</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/building-good-communication\">good communication</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/from-linguistic-to-communicative-competence\">communicative competence</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/formality-register-and-idiolect\">formality, register, idiolect</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/sixteen-components-and-the-speaking-acronym\">SPEAKING</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"re-sume-star-and-interviews-12-6-12-10\">Résumé, STAR and interviews (12.6–12.10)</h3>\n<p>A <strong>CV</strong> (<em>curriculum vitae</em>) is your full history; a <strong>résumé</strong> is its short version, “one to two page” (12.6). Its sections: name and contact; an <strong>overview</strong>; publications; research experience; awards; professional experience; education and skills; positions of responsibility. In the overview, put the institution and the year of graduation in bold; leave out self-praise (“result-oriented and performance-driven” is “not required”: “it is for the CV to speak”); give exact numbers (“more than five years” could mean twenty); do not confuse passion with expertise; use varied verbs (<em>worked, conducted, rated, tested</em>); and aim at the position (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=shVQy2W_Izs&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The STAR method (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:20</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A way to answer <strong>behavioural</strong> questions (questions about how you handled a work situation) with a real example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>ituation: the context, as specific as possible;</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>ask: your responsibility in it;</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>ction: what <strong>you</strong> did (“Instead of saying, ‘We did xyz,’ say ‘I did xyz’”);</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>esult: the outcome of your action.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“How do you deal with a tight deadline?” (the lecture’s answer at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Izrp2Vdgphk&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Situation</strong>: <em>Once, at a former company, an employee left days before the imminent deadline of one of his projects.</em> (<em>Imminent</em>: about to happen.)</li>\n<li><strong>Task</strong>: <em>I was asked to assume responsibility for it, with only a few days to learn about and complete the project.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Action</strong>: <em>I created a task force and delegated work,</em> (a <em>task force</em> is a small team for one job; to <em>delegate</em> is to hand out parts of the work)</li>\n<li><strong>Result</strong>: <em>and we all completed the assignment with a day to spare.</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the STAR story sits between an opening line about how you usually work and a closing line: <em>In fact, I believe I thrive when working under tight deadlines.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Show your <strong>suitability</strong> (why you are the best person for this job), not your <strong>eligibility</strong> (the bare minimum that every candidate has). Leave keywords, “breadcrumbs”, that the interviewer will ask about, and prepare for them. Talk about achievements, not job titles: <em>I have led a group of 300 people.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Interviews</strong> (12.8, 12.9) are “impressionistic”: in a few minutes you must make an impression. The challenges come as a chain (anxiety, nervousness, over-caution, hypercorrection, behind them a lack of preparation), and preparation breaks it. <strong>Before</strong>: relax and be yourself, research the organisation, practise the predictable questions, “package” your achievements, groom well, arrange your papers. <strong>During</strong>: answer intelligibly and confidently at a normal speed; paraphrase if they did not follow you, and ask for clarification if you did not follow them; no bad-mouthing, no categorical remarks. <strong>After</strong>: follow up and complete the formalities (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eykZsE5A02Q&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li>An interview has four parts: <strong>ice-breaking</strong>, the <strong>technical</strong> round, <strong>adaptability</strong>, and <strong>winding up</strong>. At the end, ask two or three good questions, never “Am I selected?”. In the mock interview, “decision making, leadership and good communication skills” was “very generic”; a specific story (finding three experienced local staff in Pune when the budget was short) was the proof (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jacrUXc1bTU&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>). “Being fluent does not equate to being a speaker with high speed”, and if you do not know an answer, say so.</li>\n<li><strong>“Tell me about yourself”</strong> (12.10): (1) a one-sentence introduction; (2) three to five sentences of achievements with proof, such as <em>I have trained more than 5000 employees of different multinational companies. Many have become repeat customers.</em>; (3) why you want this job. Leave out family and hobbies unless they are asked about, superlatives (“the best coder in the country”) and complaints about your present company. Make it a conversation, not a <strong>monologue</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y4SvIzrUIOQ&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the week page:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w12/writing-a-re-sume\">writing a résumé</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/the-overview\">the overview</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/position-yourself-to-win-the-star-method\">the STAR method</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/dos-and-don-ts\">dos and don’ts</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/the-challenges\">the challenges</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/three-stages\">three stages</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/before-the-interview\">before</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/during-the-interview\">during</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/after-the-interview\">after</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/linguistic-skills-interactive-skills-and-strategies\">skills and strategies</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/demonstration-1-an-english-teacher\">mock interview 1</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/demonstration-2-a-project-manager\">mock interview 2</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/what-the-board-was-looking-for\">what the board wanted</a> · <a href=\"#/english2/w12/tell-me-about-yourself\">tell me about yourself</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common mistakes in Week 12</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Thinking accuracy is enough: the context decides what a sentence means.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>Dear Dr Karen</em>; an empty subject line; a reply in anger; a forward with no word of context.</li>\n<li>✗ <em>Best Regards</em> → <em>Best regards</em>.</li>\n<li>Self-praise and vague numbers in a résumé (<em>hard-working</em>, <em>more than five years</em>).</li>\n<li><em>We did</em> in the Action of a STAR answer → <em>I did</em>.</li>\n<li>Talking about eligibility instead of suitability, repeating the CV, or answering vaguely (“I am very hard working, I got good marks”).</li>\n<li>Asking “Am I selected?”; guessing at a question you did not understand; speaking fast to sound fluent.</li>\n<li>Using many adjectives, adverbs or passives to “impress”.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Full revision:</strong> <a href=\"#/english2/w12\">Week 12</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-it-all-fits-together\">How it all fits together</h2>\n<p>The twelve weeks are not separate topics. These threads run through them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One sentence machine.</strong> Every grammar lecture uses the same template: sentence = subject + predicate; predicate = verb + objects + adjuncts; the subject and the verb are joined by agreement and tense (Weeks 3, 6 and 10). Most rules are about where the tense goes (the first table below).</li>\n<li><strong>Clauses inside clauses.</strong> Week 3 named the clauses. Week 6 put questions and <em>that</em>, <em>whether</em> and <em>if</em> clauses into the object slot. Week 9 gave the commas for each pattern. Week 10 showed that reported speech embeds a clause and that a conditional is a complex sentence. Week 11 joined clauses with contrast words and connectors. Week 4 used the same patterns to argue (<em>If … then</em>, <em>While …</em>, <em>Whereas …</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Accuracy and appropriateness.</strong> “We reach fluency through accuracy” (Week 1). Week 2 names linguistic, communicative and pragmatic competence; Week 6 adds that accuracy plus appropriateness makes us impactful; Weeks 10 and 12 explain that grammar and use are learnt together; and Week 12 shows, with barriers and the SPEAKING model, why grammar alone is not enough.</li>\n<li><strong>Softening.</strong> Polite forms come back in almost every week (the third table): modals (Week 5), softer requests, refusals and embedded questions (Week 6), the language of a group discussion (Week 7), reported speech and conditionals (Week 10), <em>a bit</em> (Week 11), saying no without <em>no</em> (Week 12).</li>\n<li><strong>Plan, draft, cut, revise.</strong> The synopsis and the 30-minute plan (Week 4), simple words and the précis drafts (Week 8), CODER and POWER (Week 9), the steps to write well and “read it three times” (Week 12): every kind of writing is planned first and revised last.</li>\n<li><strong>Be specific: show, do not label.</strong> Vague words hide the truth in writing that influences (Week 4) and in numbers (Week 7). An SOP draws a picture instead of a label (Week 8). A résumé gives exact figures, a STAR answer a real story, and “Tell me about yourself” the proof (Week 12).</li>\n<li><strong>The sounds of English.</strong> Stress, rhythm and intonation (Week 1) return in numbers (thir-<strong>TEEN</strong>, Week 7), in joined sounds and vowel length (<em>fast train</em>, <em>sin/seen</em>, Week 9) and in the pronunciation of <em>précis</em> (PRAY-see, Week 8).</li>\n<li><strong>Words.</strong> Figures of speech (Week 1), adjective endings (Week 2), the words that travel with numbers (Week 7), the three sides of a word (form, meaning and context: Weeks 9 and 12), and morphemes, word formation and loanwords (Week 11).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"where-the-tense-goes\">Where the tense goes</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Construction</th>\n<th>What happens to the tense</th>\n<th>Example from the lectures</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>statement</td>\n<td>on the verb, or on the first auxiliary</td>\n<td><em>Raju loves pizza.</em> <em>Raju was coming to Chennai.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>negative (Weeks 6, 10)</td>\n<td>leaves the verb: tense + <em>not</em> + verb</td>\n<td><em>John does not like novels.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>question (Week 6)</td>\n<td>goes to the front with agreement; <em>do</em> carries it</td>\n<td><em>What does Raju love?</em> <em>Is Raju coming to Chennai tomorrow?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>question tag (Week 6)</td>\n<td>the same tense, the opposite form, at the end</td>\n<td><em>You speak English, don’t you?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>embedded question (Week 6)</td>\n<td>does <strong>not</strong> move</td>\n<td><em>I asked what you ate in the evening.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>reported speech (Week 10)</td>\n<td>moves one step back</td>\n<td><em>Raman said that he would join us for the meeting.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>modal (Week 5)</td>\n<td>a modal has no tense, and needs no <em>do</em></td>\n<td><em>Raju cannot eat pizza.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>passive (Weeks 2, 10)</td>\n<td>sits on <em>be</em>, before V3</td>\n<td><em>The classes were suspended.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>participle, gerund (Week 10)</td>\n<td>none</td>\n<td><em>a torn shirt</em>; <em>Swimming is fun.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>non-finite clause (Weeks 3, 6)</td>\n<td>none</td>\n<td><em>Raju wants to go home.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"joining-two-ideas\">Joining two ideas</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>To join</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Example from the lectures</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>two full sentences, neutrally</td>\n<td>a comma + <em>and, but, or, so</em> (Week 9)</td>\n<td><em>He kicked the ball, and they won the match.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>two closely related sentences</td>\n<td>a semicolon, with no conjunction (Week 9)</td>\n<td><em>I thought it was terrible; they thought it was great.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a contrast, formally</td>\n<td><em>; however,</em> (Weeks 9, 11)</td>\n<td><em>You could fly via Singapore; however, this isn’t the only way.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a result</td>\n<td><em>As a result,</em> or <em>; consequently,</em> (Weeks 2, 11)</td>\n<td><em>There was no AC in the building. As a result, the workers had to be sent home.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a contrast inside one sentence</td>\n<td><em>although, though, even though</em> + a clause (Week 11)</td>\n<td><em>Vaiga didn’t come to the party even though I invited her.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a contrast before a noun phrase</td>\n<td><em>despite, in spite of</em> + a phrase (Week 11)</td>\n<td><em>Reva decided to buy an expensive car despite her mother’s objections.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a surprising fact, in a new sentence</td>\n<td><em>Even so,</em> (Week 11)</td>\n<td><em>Even so, their political influence should not be underestimated.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a condition</td>\n<td>an <em>if</em>-clause, with a comma only when it comes first (Week 10)</td>\n<td><em>If Naina rings me, I will invite her for lunch at home.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a description of a noun</td>\n<td>a relative clause, with commas only when it is non-essential (Weeks 3, 9)</td>\n<td><em>People love to celebrate New Year’s Eve, which is the last day of the year.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a list or an explanation after a full sentence</td>\n<td>a colon (Week 9)</td>\n<td><em>Never forget this point: think before you speak.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"saying-it-politely\">Saying it politely</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Polite forms from the lectures</th>\n<th>Week</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a request</td>\n<td><em>Could we meet at 9 in the morning, please?</em> · <em>I was wondering if we could meet at 9 in the morning.</em></td>\n<td>5, 6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>permission</td>\n<td><em>May I take a tea break?</em> · <em>Would you mind if I borrowed your bike for a couple of days?</em></td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an offer</td>\n<td><em>Would you like some coffee?</em> · <em>Why don’t you sit?</em></td>\n<td>5, 12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a suggestion</td>\n<td><em>We could try Thai food this time.</em> · <em>If I were you, I would think several times about it.</em></td>\n<td>5, 6, 10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a question</td>\n<td><em>I wonder what her name is.</em> · <em>Could you tell me if the doctor is available?</em></td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a criticism</td>\n<td><em>You seem to have missed a few things. May I suggest a revisit?</em></td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a refusal</td>\n<td><em>I have a lot of stuff pending to take care of. Hope you won’t mind.</em> · <em>I have to go shopping.</em></td>\n<td>6, 12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an unexpected call</td>\n<td><em>If it wasn’t urgent, I wouldn’t have called you today.</em></td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a softer statement</td>\n<td><em>I was a bit bored at the concert.</em> · <em>The delivery may be delayed.</em></td>\n<td>5, 11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a group discussion</td>\n<td><em>Shall we try this idea?</em> · <em>Can I have a chance?</em></td>\n<td>7</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"which-piece-of-writing\">Which piece of writing</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Writing (week)</th>\n<th>Its job</th>\n<th>The key rule</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>argument essay (4)</td>\n<td>two sides of a disputed question</td>\n<td>situation, one side, the other side, conclusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>lab manual (4)</td>\n<td>instructions before the work</td>\n<td>things, actions, sequence, duration, changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>reflective essay (4)</td>\n<td>a question with no single answer</td>\n<td>a synopsis first; 5 + 20 + 5 minutes; stories</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>statement of purpose (8)</td>\n<td>your own story</td>\n<td>show, do not label; simple words</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>précis (8)</td>\n<td>a passage in one-third of its words</td>\n<td>keep every point; one paragraph</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>lab report (8)</td>\n<td>a record after the work</td>\n<td>passive voice, past tense, impersonal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>letter of request (9)</td>\n<td>asking for a service</td>\n<td>POWER; formal and polite</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>research report (12)</td>\n<td>research put in writing</td>\n<td>problem, method, evidence, conclusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>e-mail (12)</td>\n<td>fast communication</td>\n<td>subject, salutation, succinct body, closing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>résumé (12)</td>\n<td>your work in one or two pages</td>\n<td>a precise overview; no self-praise</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The course’s other “which form when” tables are in the weeks above: tense and aspect (Week 2), modals (Week 5), conditionals (Week 10) and quantifiers (Week 11).</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Turn <em>The principal suspended the classes</em> into the passive. What is the subject, what is the agent, and what is the job of <em>by the principal</em>? In which kind of writing is the passive the normal style?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>The classes are the focus, so they take the subject position: <em>The classes …</em></li>\n<li>Keep the past tense on <em>be</em>: past + plural = <em>were</em>. Then add V3: <em>suspended</em>.</li>\n<li>Add the agent only if you want it: <em>by the principal</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>The classes were suspended (by the principal).</em> The subject is <em>the classes</em>; the agent is <em>the principal</em>; <em>by the principal</em> is an optional <strong>adjunct</strong>, not an object (Lecture 10.1). The passive is the normal style of a <strong>lab report</strong> (Week 8), because the doer does not matter there: anyone who follows the procedure should get the same result. Week 12 warns only against using too many passives elsewhere.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Start from <em>Raju ate thin pizza in the evening.</em> Make it negative, add a tag, ask about the object, put that question after <em>I asked</em>, and report <em>Ravi said, “Raju ate thin pizza in the evening.”</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Negative: the past leaves the verb, and <em>do</em> carries it: <em>Raju <strong>did not eat</strong> thin pizza in the evening.</em></li>\n<li>Tag: the same subject (<em>he</em>), the same tense (<em>did</em>), the opposite form, contracted: <em>Raju ate thin pizza in the evening, <strong>didn’t he</strong>?</em></li>\n<li>Question: the past and the agreement go to the front on <em>did</em>, and the verb is bare: <em><strong>What did Raju eat</strong> in the evening?</em></li>\n<li>Embedded: inside a bigger sentence nothing moves: <em>I asked <strong>what Raju ate</strong> in the evening.</em> (✗ <em>what did Raju eat</em>)</li>\n<li>Reported: the simple past moves back to the past perfect: <em>Ravi said that Raju <strong>had eaten</strong> thin pizza in the evening.</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Weeks 6 and 10 in one sentence: the tense leaves the verb (a negative), goes to the front (a question), stays where it is (an embedded question), or moves back (reported speech).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Fill in the verbs and name the type: (a) <em>If I ___ (be) you, I ___ (think) several times about it.</em> (b) <em>If I had studied harder, I ___ (pass) the exam.</em> (c) <em>If it rains tomorrow, I ___ (stay) at home.</em> (d) <em>If you add one and one, you ___ (get) two.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <em><strong>were</strong></em>, <em><strong>would think</strong></em>: second conditional (unreal: I am not you), with <em>were</em> for every subject. It is also a soft suggestion (Weeks 6 and 10).</li>\n<li>(b) <em><strong>would have passed</strong></em>: third conditional, an imagined past (past perfect + <em>would have</em> + V3).</li>\n<li>(c) <em><strong>will stay</strong></em> (<em>'ll stay</em>): first conditional, a real future possibility (present + <em>will</em>).</li>\n<li>(d) <em><strong>get</strong></em>: zero conditional, a fact (present + present, with no modal).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All four are the lectures’ own sentences (Lectures 5.3 and 6.1).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Analyse the clauses and add the commas: (a) <em>My cousin Nisha whom you met at my home is heading to US tomorrow.</em> (b) <em>If you get good grades I will take you to London.</em> (c) <em>He was so happy when he won the match!</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Verbs with tense: <em>met</em>, <em>is heading</em>: two clauses. The main clause is <em>My cousin Nisha is heading to US tomorrow</em>; <em>whom you met at my home</em> is an <strong>adjectival (relative)</strong> clause qualifying <em>Nisha</em>. Without it the sentence still makes sense, so it is <strong>non-essential</strong>, and in the middle it takes two commas: <em>My cousin Nisha, whom you met at my home, is heading to US tomorrow.</em></li>\n<li>(b) <em>If you get good grades</em> is a dependent (<strong>adverbial</strong>) clause, and it comes first: D + I → a comma, and no <em>and</em>: <em>If you get good grades, I will take you to London.</em></li>\n<li>(c) The independent clause comes first, then the dependent <em>when</em>-clause: I + D → <strong>no</strong> comma. The sentence is right as it is.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(Weeks 3 and 9.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Join <em>I invited Vaiga to the party.</em> and <em>She didn’t come.</em> in four correct ways, and give one wrong way.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>A comma and a conjunction: <em>I invited Vaiga to the party, but she didn’t come.</em> (The lecture’s slide has no comma; Week 9 puts one before a conjunction that joins two independent clauses.)</li>\n<li><em>Although</em> + a clause, first (D + I, so a comma): <em>Although I invited Vaiga to the party, she didn’t come.</em></li>\n<li><em>Even though</em>, with the expectation in focus (I + D, no comma): <em>Vaiga didn’t come to the party even though I invited her.</em> This one shows disappointment.</li>\n<li>Formally: <em>I invited Vaiga to the party; however, she did not come.</em> There is no contraction in formal writing.</li>\n<li>Wrong: ✗ <em>I invited Vaiga to the party, she didn’t come.</em> A comma alone between two independent clauses is a <strong>comma splice</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(Weeks 9 and 11.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Correct each sentence and name the rule: (a) <em>I very agree with your decision.</em> (b) <em>Most of work has been completed.</em> (c) <em>A little teachers will accompany the students.</em> (d) <em>Neither of the pens were working.</em> (e) <em>Raju can ate pizza.</em> (f) <em>Miss Maya has set the question paper. The exam would be very tough.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <em>I <strong>very much</strong> agree with your decision.</em> <em>Very much</em> goes with feeling verbs; <em>very</em> alone never goes before a verb (Week 2).</li>\n<li>(b) <em>Most work</em> (in general) or <em>Most <strong>of the</strong> work</em> (in particular): <em>of</em> comes only before <em>the</em> (Week 11).</li>\n<li>(c) <em><strong>A few</strong> teachers</em>: <em>teachers</em> is countable, and <em>a little</em> is for uncountable nouns (Week 11).</li>\n<li>(d) <em>Neither of the pens <strong>was</strong> working</em>: <em>neither</em> takes a singular verb (Week 11).</li>\n<li>(e) <em>Raju can <strong>eat</strong> pizza</em>: a modal has no tense, so the bare verb follows it (Week 5).</li>\n<li>(f) <em>The exam <strong>could</strong> be very tough</em>: for a possible situation use <em>could</em>, never <em>would</em> (Week 5).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">A report says: “78.1% of the total 350 respondents showed preference towards personal car or two-wheeler.” How many people is that, how would you say the share on the radio, and what should a careful reader ask?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>People: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.781</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">350</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">273.35</span></span></span></span>,</span> about 273 (Lecture 4.4).</li>\n<li>On the radio, compare the share with a simple fraction (Lecture 7.2). Three quarters is 75%, and 78.1 is a little above it, so “a little over three quarters”. (The sentence is mine, made with the lecture’s method.)</li>\n<li>Say the numbers slowly; the sample is “three hundred <strong>and</strong> fifty” (Lecture 7.3).</li>\n<li>Ask “<strong>of how many</strong>?”: 350 people is a tiny sample when a single big city has lakhs of car owners, and a <em>preference</em> is not ownership (Lecture 4.4).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> about 273 people: “a little over three quarters” of a small sample of 350.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">The lecture cut <em>My seventeen years of teaching experience and the ability to lead and control various departments can surely add value to your esteemed organization</em> to <em>I taught for seventeen years. I can work with different kinds of people.</em> Count the words, and say what was cut and why.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Before: 23 words. After: 13 words. Saved: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">23</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">13</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li><em>My seventeen years of teaching experience</em> became a verb, <em>I taught for seventeen years</em>: the sentence was <strong>rewritten</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>lead and control</em>: one of the two would do, so one is <strong>redundant</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>surely</em> adds nothing: also redundant.</li>\n<li><em>add value to your esteemed organization</em> is a <strong>cliché</strong>; the rewrite says what the writer can actually do.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 23 → 13 words with the same message. These are the cuts of Week 4 and the précis techniques (a), removing redundant words, and (c), rewriting sentences, of Week 8.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What does each reply mean, and which competence lets the listener understand it? (a) To a visitor: <em>Why don’t you sit?</em> (b) To “You said you would buy me an ice cream”: <em>When did I say that?</em> (c) To “Let us go watch a movie today”: <em>I have to go shopping.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A question in form; in use, an <strong>offer</strong> of a seat (Lecture 12.4).</li>\n<li>(b) A question in form; in use, a <strong>denial</strong>, “I did not say so”, like the Hindi <em>maiN-ne kab kahaa?</em> (Lecture 12.1).</li>\n<li>(c) A statement about shopping; in use, a polite <strong>refusal</strong> (Lecture 12.1; compare the skilful refusals of Lecture 6.1).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All three are understood through <strong>pragmatic competence</strong>, knowing what a sentence means in its context. Grammar alone (linguistic competence) would read (a) and (b) as plain questions.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Asked “Why should we hire you?”, a candidate says: “Sir, because I am very hard working, I got good marks.” What is wrong, and how do Weeks 8 and 12 fix it?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>It is <strong>vague</strong>: it is the lecture’s own example of what not to say (Lecture 12.7). <em>Hard working</em> is a label without evidence, and “hard work” is subjective (Lecture 8.1).</li>\n<li>Good marks show <strong>eligibility</strong>, which every shortlisted candidate has; the answer should show <strong>suitability</strong> (Lecture 12.7).</li>\n<li>Give the proof as a <strong>STAR</strong> story, like the lecture’s: the Situation (an employee left days before a deadline), the Task (I was asked to take over the project), the Action (<em>I</em> created a task force and delegated work) and the Result (we finished with a day to spare).</li>\n<li>Add a number where you can, such as <em>I have led a group of 300 people</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> show, do not label, as in the SOP: a specific story and a number prove what the label only claims.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Name the figure of speech or the word-formation process: (a) <em>The White House wished all Indians on the occasion of Diwali.</em> (b) <em>Can we have a headcount?</em> (c) <em>brunch</em> (d) <em>exam</em> (e) <em>NATO</em>. Then split <em>unhappy</em> and <em>books</em> into morphemes.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Metonymy</strong>: the White House is a place closely related to the President, not a part of him (Week 1).</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>Synecdoche</strong>: <em>head</em>, a part, stands for the whole person (Week 1).</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Blending</strong>: <em>br(eakfast)</em> + <em>(l)unch</em> (Week 11).</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>Back clipping</strong>: the end of <em>examination</em> is cut off (Week 11).</li>\n<li>(e) <strong>Acronymy</strong>: North Atlantic Treaty Organisation (Week 11).</li>\n<li><em>unhappy</em> = <em>un-</em> (a derivational prefix: a new word with the opposite meaning) + <em>happy</em> (a free lexical morpheme). <em>books</em> = <em>book</em> + plural <em>-s</em> (an inflection: the same noun, in the plural).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Correct this e-mail: it has no subject line and reads “hey Dr Karen, can you send the marks report now its urgent. Your’s sincerely Sam”.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Subject</strong>: never empty: <em>Reg. Marks Report</em> (Week 12).</li>\n<li><strong>Salutation</strong>: <em>hey</em> is very informal, and a title goes with the last name: <em>Dear Dr Jones,</em> (or <em>Dear Karen,</em>), followed by an empty line (Week 12).</li>\n<li><strong>Request</strong>: <em>could</em> is more polite than <em>can</em>, and a reason helps: <em>Could you please send me the marks report? I would like to take it to my meeting with Rachel tomorrow.</em> (Weeks 5 and 12)</li>\n<li><strong>Apostrophes</strong>: <em>its urgent</em> means “it is urgent”, so <em>It’s urgent</em>. And <em>yours</em> never takes an apostrophe (Week 9).</li>\n<li><strong>Closing</strong>: for an e-mail, a short closing such as <em>Best regards,</em> then the name <em>Sam</em>, with no comma after it (Week 12).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer</strong> (each / marks a new line): under the subject <em>Reg. Marks Report</em>: <em>Dear Dr Jones, / Could you please send me the marks report? I would like to take it to my meeting with Rachel tomorrow. It’s urgent. / Best regards, / Sam</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
